WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.220
- So welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.860
I just watched the movie,
"The Ice Cream Sellers".

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.530
I hope you've had a chance to watch it.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.876
It's an absolutely fantastic,
really eye opening,

00:00:17.876 --> 00:00:22.876
really unflinching view of
the Cox's Bazar refugees.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.110
And I'm here today with
Sohel Rahman, the filmmaker,

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.170
to have a Q&amp;A and discussion.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.730
I'll ask him a few questions,

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.910
but hopefully we can
get a lot more questions

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.080
from the audience.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.426
So to introduce myself,

00:00:38.426 --> 00:00:41.940
I'm Neela Saldanha, and
I'm the Executive Director

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.120
of the Yale Research Initiative
on Innovation and Scale,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.530
that's Y-RISE.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.270
And the faculty director of Y-RISE

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.870
is Professor Mushfiq Mobarak
in the Economics Department.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.660
At Y-RISE, we look at
advancing the science

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.770
of scaling development
and policy interventions.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.580
And humanitarian issues such as refugee

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.570
and conflict areas is always important

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:11.530
because in some ways,
unlike regular poverty,

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:13.890
I shouldn't say regular,

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.000
but really about ongoing
and chronic poverty issues.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Humanitarian crisis can often
exacerbate what's going on.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.540
As in the case of Covid-19,
the world goes out of gear.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.460
We've seen that with any emergency,

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.000
so it's even worse when you
have a humanitarian crisis.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.620
What I think is actually depressing

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.190
but also familiar about crises

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.230
such as the Rohingya crisis

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:47.670
is that it is also a chronic
longstanding problem.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.730
We'll talk a little bit
about that in the Q&amp;A.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.160
It's my great pleasure
to welcome Sohel Rahman.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.060
Sohel is an award-winning filmmaker,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.560
writer and producer.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.290
He's currently based in Lisbon, Portugal.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.330
His films have been
screened internationally

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.960
in various film festivals and
universities around the world.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.200
Sohel received the Best
Feature Documentary Film Award

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.230
from the South Asian Film
Festival of Montreal, Canada

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:18.230
in 2021 and from Tasveer South
Asian Film Festival, Seattle

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.470
in 2021 for this film,
"The Ice Cream Sellers".

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.240
He also received the Best Film Award

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.290
from Lusofona University, Lisbon in 2015

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.690
for his film, "A Passagem" (The Passage).

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.880
In the same year, he won
the Special Jury Award

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.669
at the Encontros International Cinema

00:02:34.669 --> 00:02:37.083
de Vienna Film Festival in Portugal.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.808
Born in Chittagong, Bangladesh,

00:02:39.808 --> 00:02:43.380
Sohel's love of visual art,
literature and storytelling drew

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.480
him to film-making early in life.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.270
He teaches film in different
universities and institutes

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.395
in Portugal and Bangladesh
as an invited filmmaker.

00:02:51.395 --> 00:02:53.730
He's the director and chief organizer

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.873
of Mostra de Cinema sul
Asiatico, Lisbon, Portugal.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.660
So welcome, Sohel.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.600
It's really an honor to
have you here talking

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.433
about your film.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.160
- Thank you so much, Neela.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.870
It's an honor to have
me here and my presence

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.300
and screen my film here.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.860
Thank you so much.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:17.693
- Thank you.

00:03:17.693 --> 00:03:22.290
And I know we have fewer
members of the audience here,

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:26.300
but I'd really like to
start with a question

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.300
around where is everybody
really coming from?

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.750
But if I ask you where you're coming from,

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:37.890
you're gonna say New Haven.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.020
So really want to know first of all,

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.050
where are our members of the audience?

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.510
Where did you actually grow
up? What were your experiences?

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:50.510
So if you can put into the
Zoom chat we currently have,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.580
which country you grew up in,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.350
if there were multiple
countries you grew up in,

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:56.850
put all of them down.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.520
I think it'll give us a
sense of what experiences

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.660
our audiences have  had because
our experiences inform

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.610
how we look at things,
like Rohingya, et cetera.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:13.610
So I'd love to know where
you actually came from.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.232
So put in your answers in the chat

00:04:15.232 --> 00:04:18.003
and let's see what shows up in the map.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.120
So I'm gonna start.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.260
I will put my answer.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.763
I grew up in India.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:29.520
And I think, Sohel, you grew up in,

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.100
so you can see that's me.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.243
Grew up in India.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.030
You don't have to raise your hand.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.200
Just put your answers down,

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.150
just type out your answers in the chat.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.773
Shanghai. It's good to know.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.083
Turkey.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:53.763
Denmark.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.280
U.S.A., New York State.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:00.113
So it's good to know.

00:05:00.113 --> 00:05:01.410
I think we've got people.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.060
And the reason I think this is important

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.559
is because where we grew up

00:05:04.559 --> 00:05:07.620
and where we spend our lives influences

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.764
and provides us a lens also
into the world, I think.

00:05:10.764 --> 00:05:14.273
So it's nice to know that folks

00:05:14.273 --> 00:05:18.630
have really come from all over the world.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:22.920
And I'd really like to know also,

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.290
have you watched the movie yet?

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:26.820
Because if not, I think Sohel

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.830
can give a little bit of an introduction

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.840
to the movie and I really
hope you'll watch it.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.370
I spent...

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.230
I didn't even realize
an hour and 15 minutes

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.300
and I had to sit in silence after that

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.670
for the next I think 10 or 15 minutes

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.730
because no matter how much
you read about something,

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.162
I think it's when you watch
the everyday lives of people

00:05:44.162 --> 00:05:47.160
who are going through
that particular situation,

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.440
you can really sort of
understand what's happening.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.560
So again, if you can
just let me know in the,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.300
maybe just put it in the Q&amp;A,

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.970
whether you've watched the movie,

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.377
the "Ice Cream Sellers".

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:03.760
Well, let me just actually share my screen

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:10.413
and let me.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:13.853
This one.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.813
Let me do this poll then.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.560
Okay, so there are three.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.330
Just type one, two or three.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.163
Like have you watched the movie?

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.220
If you've seen the the whole movie,

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.380
if you've only partly seen the movie,

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.050
you started watching 10
minutes and then you stopped

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.700
or if you haven't watched it now.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.850
Just put in one, two,
or three on the chat.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.263
Great, Dean.

00:06:42.263 --> 00:06:45.090
Yes, it was really eyeopening, I think.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:45.923
So,

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:48.443
yeah, that's great.

00:06:48.443 --> 00:06:51.330
So some of you watched it partly, I think.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:55.380
Yeah, looks like people
have watched it, Sohel.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.053
And even though this is
a relatively small group,

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:00.573
it's good to see.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.490
Yeah, so it looks like a lot of you

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.020
have watched the movie.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.262
It's not long for the ones who said partly

00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:11.280
or those who say no.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.273
It's just an hour and 15 minutes.

00:07:14.273 --> 00:07:16.740
And I think it's
absolutely worth watching.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.510
I didn't even do what I normally do,

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:21.990
which is to put this on 1.25
speed or one and a half speed

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:25.245
because I think it's really
not just the film itself,

00:07:25.245 --> 00:07:28.470
but the silences and some of the scenes

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:32.610
that are worth actually
taking in and reflecting on.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.640
So I actually hope that everybody

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:39.450
has actually sort of watched the movie

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:43.410
or ends up watching it as a
result of our discussion today

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.140
and questions that you
might have and so on.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.949
So for those of you who
actually watched the movie,

00:07:49.949 --> 00:07:52.500
what sort of, again,
you can put in the chat,

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.720
what sort of words would
you use to describe,

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.330
and this is, I think, Sohel
was looking for some feedback

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.163
on the movie.

00:07:58.163 --> 00:08:01.680
So my quick question before
we start and I ask him,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.240
what sort of words would you use

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.117
to describe the "Ice Cream Sellers"?

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:10.780
You can just put it in the chat,

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.970
four or five of you who
have watched the movie.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.610
Yeah, I think that was true.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:21.933
It was very moving.

00:08:22.863 --> 00:08:24.273
I felt the same way.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:31.277
And the other,

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:37.740
unfair.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.560
That's very interesting
and maybe I'll call on you

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.693
to talk about that a little bit.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:50.550
Just any words that come to mind.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.320
I know a lot of words came to mind.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.830
Not all of them can be
repeated because like you,

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.520
I found it unfair and
it made me angry a bit

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.700
to see the unfairness.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.132
But any other words that
for those of you who watched

00:09:05.132 --> 00:09:08.733
the movie and if someone said
it was a great experience.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:15.570
Playful. That's interesting.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.030
Yes, I think I got a
sense of that as well.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:19.890
And maybe we'll probe a little bit

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.830
with Sohel in that candid.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:24.810
Evocative, yes.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:28.080
I think the games and
toys really led to that.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.960
And so, it's kind of an
unusual combination, I agree,

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.970
with sort of the general sentiment here

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:34.878
that not only was it moving,

00:09:34.878 --> 00:09:37.770
but it was also moving
because it was playful

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.780
and because of the games and toys.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.400
So, in some ways,

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.336
and we talk a little bit about that,

00:09:43.336 --> 00:09:46.560
what led Sohel to make this kind of movie

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.980
and this particular lens
on things that we have.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.050
But, yeah, thank you very much.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:57.050
And I'll sort of stop
sharing and get to...

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.550
I hope that was good and
you sort of saw a sense

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:05.220
of Sohel leaving a sense of
what the audience took away

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.560
from the movie, which is what
I took away from the movie,

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.726
this sort of sense of both
being moving and playful,

00:10:11.726 --> 00:10:16.726
of being, astounded is
a good word, I think.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.530
Astounded by both the resilience,

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:23.280
but the matter of factness
really came out to me as well,

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.550
which is that often when
people have suffered so much,

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:30.930
there is just no way to
describe the reaction

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.750
other than being completely
matter of fact about it.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.795
And if you actually mute the
sound and you just listen

00:10:35.795 --> 00:10:38.280
to them, you wouldn't think
that they were talking

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:40.862
about their husbands or their sons

00:10:40.862 --> 00:10:44.430
or their daughters being
killed or raped or mutilated.

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:46.800
You'd just think they were
talking about everyday things.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.600
And I don't know whether that was,

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.150
for me that was even more sort of moving

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.030
than if that had been drama.

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:54.980
But I want to come to
sort of this question

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.867
of what motivated you to make this film

00:10:57.867 --> 00:11:01.620
and this particular angle
of using the children

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.950
and using those elements
of joy and playfulness

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.080
in the middle of this terrible,

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:09.107
portraying this atrocity, Sohel.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:14.130
- Okay, thank you, Neela, for
giving me a chance to explain

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:17.850
and thank you all audience who
participated in the chatting

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:19.593
and also saying this feedback.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:23.343
Is great to hear from all of you.

00:11:24.468 --> 00:11:29.283
Well, first of all, when it's
started happening in 2017,

00:11:29.283 --> 00:11:31.740
my mom was calling me all the time.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:33.217
Every day was like,

00:11:33.217 --> 00:11:35.751
"What's happening here in the Naf River?"

00:11:35.751 --> 00:11:38.400
'Cause I'm from Chittagong,
and it's not far

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.611
from the refugee camp in Bangladesh.

00:11:41.611 --> 00:11:45.865
It's a few hours from my
hometown to the refugee camp.

00:11:45.865 --> 00:11:49.230
And all of our people from the country,

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:51.780
Bangladeshi people,
they went there to help

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.878
Whatever they had, they extended help

00:11:53.878 --> 00:11:58.740
like whoever needed some water
or some food or anything,

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.000
whatever they had.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.220
And my mom was saying like,
"Oh, what are you doing?

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:03.870
You have to do something for them."

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:06.350
And I felt like, yeah,
I have to do something

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:07.220
in what I can do.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:10.857
So I was following up the
news, what's really happening,

00:12:10.857 --> 00:12:13.830
and one picture just touched my heart.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.080
It just broke my heart.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.070
A mother, she was carrying
two children on her shoulder

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:24.570
and running fast towards the border.

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:28.110
So it's like, my mother
doesn't belong to the Rohingya,

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:31.770
but what would have
happen if she if she did?

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.680
Then I just imagine a
situation that it is my mom

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:38.607
and carrying me and my siblings

00:12:38.607 --> 00:12:41.220
and also running towards the border.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.402
And I can't imagine that.

00:12:43.402 --> 00:12:46.809
I just felt like, okay,
so I have to do something

00:12:46.809 --> 00:12:49.890
as a human, as a filmmaker.

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:53.100
So I flew from Lisbon to Chittagong,

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:57.480
and I went to that refugee
camp and started filming.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.623
So first of all, I was
like a little bit lost

00:13:00.623 --> 00:13:04.440
because there's too much for someone

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:08.669
to endure all those happening,
all those suffering.

00:13:08.669 --> 00:13:12.390
Or you see the wounded people, women,

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:14.550
and they cry and are traumatized.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:18.510
They almost lost
everything, even the sense

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:20.430
and normal sensibility.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:24.570
So when they were talking,
they feel like talking.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.870
They were narrating,

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:31.975
they were describing a killing
like they have breakfast.

00:13:31.975 --> 00:13:34.290
They're talking about the breakfast,

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.513
they're talking something
like usual things.

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:40.740
I saw this, they lost this.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:44.550
Then I was kind of distracted
with the storytelling

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:47.400
because I cannot just shoot randomly.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.320
So I need a story to tell.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.412
And I just felt like, no,
I have to tell this story

00:13:51.412 --> 00:13:56.220
because I have this access
and I know the dialect,

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:00.390
what they speak and is very
essential for a filmmaker

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.480
when you go there, and you
cannot just tell their story

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:04.800
if you don't have the access,

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:08.867
if you don't have the same
language or same empathy

00:14:08.867 --> 00:14:11.550
or the suffering or pain.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.050
And in that case,

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:16.380
first couple of days I
spend for my research like,

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.000
okay, what would be my film?

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.010
What would be my story?

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:22.530
And I didn't want to make a
conventional refugee film.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.090
I know that there are a lot of film

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:25.618
and everybody understands,

00:14:25.618 --> 00:14:28.560
okay, it's the cry, it's the suffering.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:30.150
So what else do we need?

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:33.810
And I just decided,
okay, my team of the film

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:38.810
would be something like
after this massive massacre,

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:43.350
bloodshed, after all this
losing their country,

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.750
their homeland, their loved one.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:50.750
So a community, how they can
really survive as a human.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:54.150
So I just saw everywhere
there are children,

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:56.100
children, children, children,

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:01.100
and on every street of
this tiny little path,

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.000
you see children.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.490
Children are children wherever they are.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:08.880
Is still, they have this
irreplaceable spirit,

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:10.350
laughter, happiness.

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:13.200
Even though there is
weakness, they saw everything,

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.887
but still they have
this spirit of children

00:15:15.887 --> 00:15:20.887
and they are trying to
make their childhood.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.430
They're trying to create
their own childhood

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:24.960
wherever they are.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:26.220
And then I was like, okay,

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:30.120
so maybe it would be nice
to have the perspective

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:33.240
through the like a post genocide period

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.160
through the eyes of children.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:35.993
How?

00:15:35.993 --> 00:15:39.240
Because those children, they
are hope, they are dream.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.940
They are the power for this community.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:43.710
That's what I felt.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.740
Then I started like,
okay, this is the story.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.071
Then I needed to find out someone

00:15:49.071 --> 00:15:51.510
to encourage, to guide me,

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:55.650
to lead the storyline,
the spine of the story.

00:15:55.650 --> 00:16:00.150
Then through several encounter,
I found Ayas and Asia,

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:01.560
these little siblings.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:03.510
And I asked them, "What are you doing?"

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:05.520
And, "Having ice cream."

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.130
Then, "Oh, okay, so you
are selling ice cream?

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:09.367
Why are you selling ice cream?"

00:16:09.367 --> 00:16:10.957
"Because we need to make money."

00:16:10.957 --> 00:16:13.200
"Why do you need to make money."

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:16.777
Then they said, "Okay, I have
to give this money to my mom."

00:16:16.777 --> 00:16:20.527
"And your mom, what she's
going to do with this money?"

00:16:20.527 --> 00:16:23.640
"Because our father is
in prison in Myanmar

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:28.640
and if we send some bribe money
to the officials in Myanmar

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.840
in prison so that our
father can get released."

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:37.944
So this is their hope and
they keep trying everyday.

00:16:37.944 --> 00:16:41.130
This is such a huge work. It's very hard.

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:42.867
Even I was with my camera.

00:16:42.867 --> 00:16:45.210
And I was with my camera,
I was getting tired.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:48.180
It's so dusty, so hot.

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:51.390
The camp is a never ending village.

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.430
You keep walking and you just need to,

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:55.830
and I was amazed.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.564
I was like, oh, my god, how
the power these teach us.

00:16:59.564 --> 00:17:01.823
They are incredible, those.
- [Neela] Right.

00:17:01.823 --> 00:17:03.540
- So that just moved me.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:07.677
And then I choose these
children to go on with this

00:17:07.677 --> 00:17:10.650
and I asked them if they allow me to film.

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:12.600
And I said, you don't have to do anything.

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:16.980
You just let me know to
film you, to follow you.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.210
That's it, I need.

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:19.680
So every day, the goal,

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:23.040
I go with them and they stop one door,

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:27.090
another door, I stop and
I face several people

00:17:27.090 --> 00:17:30.630
and I encounter those people
and they tell me their story.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:31.980
There are several testimonies.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:34.260
I didn't put everything in the film.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:35.460
And I tried.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:39.900
It is very hard also in
the post-production time

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:42.033
because which one is more important?

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.130
I have to make a balance
because there's so heavy things

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:50.130
and I can't deal with heaviness.

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:52.680
I needed to make it a bit lighter.

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.086
And I see this.

00:17:54.086 --> 00:17:58.020
On one hand, we have
harrowing tale of parents.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:02.820
On the other hand, the children,
their laughter, their joy.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:04.403
So that's what...

00:18:04.403 --> 00:18:05.549
Yeah, sorry.

00:18:05.549 --> 00:18:07.743
- That's fantastic.

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:12.155
I just wanna call attention
to one thing that you said,

00:18:12.155 --> 00:18:13.950
which I think is so true.

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:16.560
So this movie hit particularly hard for me

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:19.132
because in a previous life,
I worked with Unilever,

00:18:19.132 --> 00:18:21.004
which sells ice creams,

00:18:21.004 --> 00:18:22.950
Kwality Wall's one of the brands.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:26.511
And I was actually responsible
for ice cream sales

00:18:26.511 --> 00:18:28.260
in a part of North India.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:30.120
And I think what you
said is completely true

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:31.918
about it's very hard work.

00:18:31.918 --> 00:18:34.620
One of the things that I
was just thinking about

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:38.610
is also, if you don't sell
the ice creams enough,

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:39.630
they melt.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:41.727
So this is not a product
like selling chips

00:18:41.727 --> 00:18:45.090
or selling something which
will last for the next day.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.190
You have to sell what you started

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:49.290
at the beginning of the
day by the end of the day

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:50.701
otherwise it's useless.

00:18:50.701 --> 00:18:53.323
And I'm sure the person that
they are buying the ice creams

00:18:53.323 --> 00:18:56.790
from will judge them if
they have unsold ice cream.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:58.080
So it's also very...

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:00.228
Holding that box, I was really wondering

00:19:00.228 --> 00:19:04.470
how the little boy was actually
sort of holding the box

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:05.760
and going around so easily.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:10.760
It's heavy and it's insulated
and it's very hard work

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:14.465
and yet they seem to do
it very matter of factly.

00:19:14.465 --> 00:19:16.860
And I do think that what
you've said came through

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:19.890
very clearly in terms of the joyfulness

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:21.973
and the playfulness contrasted

00:19:21.973 --> 00:19:24.933
with the subject being so heavy.

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:27.840
And I noticed that you used
that in a number of places.

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.120
But before that, there was
a very interesting question

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.250
on CC Young, which is, "Hi, Sohel.

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:33.930
Your documentary's really powerful?

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.880
Can you give us a bit of
background about the meaning

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.830
of ice cream and ice in the area

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:39.597
where you shot the documentary?"

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:43.800
Especially there was a lot of imagery

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:47.100
about people taking baths,

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.350
bucket baths, washing
their bodies with water.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:51.189
So was there something
there that connected it

00:19:51.189 --> 00:19:55.320
or how did you get on the story

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:57.060
of the kids selling ice cream?

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.560
We saw a lot of other vendors.

00:19:58.560 --> 00:19:59.940
Someone was selling earrings,

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:01.140
someone was selling balloons.

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:02.460
Of course, they're adult vendors.

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:04.500
But how did you get into?

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:06.720
Was there anything specific
about the ice cream

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.243
or it was just one of those?

00:20:10.176 --> 00:20:14.970
- Well, this ice, when I
was working, I was filming.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:16.950
Thanks for the question.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:19.620
Is a very interesting question

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:23.760
and I would love to talk about
this because it was very hot

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:26.310
and they don't have any
power, any electricity

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:29.520
and they don't have any
fridge and any cold water.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:33.266
And I was sweating even me
when I was with the camera

00:20:33.266 --> 00:20:37.057
and everybody is very,
the shelter of the...

00:20:37.057 --> 00:20:41.310
Is not a home, but the
shelter of the refugee camp

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:42.270
in the camp.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:45.390
So it's very dusty and hot.

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:48.420
So they don't have any
cold water or anything.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:51.840
Even also that they're
not surrounded by trees

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:53.250
or green things.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.366
Now it's growing these days.

00:20:55.366 --> 00:20:58.560
But when I was filming, it
was right after the genocide.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.750
It's very new and very fresh.

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:04.850
That time, I just felt
like, wow, this ice cream,

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:08.313
these kids, they are not
just selling ice cream.

00:21:09.655 --> 00:21:14.340
They are putting a little
piece of cold water.

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:21.560
Is like a piece giving them
peace in that burning fire.

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:24.090
So you give a little water.

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:25.190
They needed something.

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:30.840
That's why these kids bringing the hope

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:32.280
through this ice cream.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:35.517
They have nothing, but
they offer you something

00:21:35.517 --> 00:21:37.590
and that's a piece of ice cream.

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:39.240
But that's a metaphor, a big,

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:43.113
that box they holds on the shoulder,

00:21:43.113 --> 00:21:45.600
they hold their lives there.

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:46.815
Like taking that,

00:21:46.815 --> 00:21:49.920
that's moved me a lot.

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:54.180
So I also, I tried several
title for the film,

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:57.630
but then I was like, no,
I wanted to make it simple

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:59.130
and short.

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:03.810
So I choose "Ice Cream Sellers"
as a title of the film.

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:05.357
- [Neela] Yeah. And-

00:22:05.357 --> 00:22:06.945
- And I think that's your film.

00:22:06.945 --> 00:22:09.510
It's simple on the surface,

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.630
but it's got a lot of layers
once you sort of unpeel

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:13.609
the surface.

00:22:13.609 --> 00:22:15.840
It seems like a simple thing.

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.270
And I did notice that at
the beginning of the film

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.630
you have this very lush sort of green,

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:23.190
as they're fleeing, I think there's

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.470
a shorter very lush green fields.

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:29.460
And then when I saw the
the camp, you're right,

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:31.140
it's very dusty.

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:33.360
In fact, halfway through I realize

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:36.300
there's not even a
single tree in this camp.

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:39.780
It's just all like dust
and it's brown big.

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:43.173
And it just brought me to mind of how,

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.540
it's not just about
being in a refugee camp,

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:47.850
which is hard enough,

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:49.770
but it's where you actually are

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:51.113
to be just stripped off nature

00:22:51.113 --> 00:22:54.180
and in such a difficult
situation must just add

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:55.860
to the difficulties.

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:58.178
One thing I noticed, Sohel, in the movie

00:22:58.178 --> 00:23:01.350
which really struck me was that

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:06.350
in this temporary area
of temporary living,

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:08.700
people maintained such good rules.

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:10.650
So halfway through, I was wondering,

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.092
there are two little
kids taking ice creams.

00:23:12.092 --> 00:23:16.770
Anyone can just sort of snatch
their ice cream box, right?

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:18.780
Or not pay for the ice creams.

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:21.573
And yet every transaction, people haggle.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:24.453
They bargain in any market.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:30.380
The kid says, "I wanna get
my profit if I sell so many."

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:32.760
It was a very normal interaction.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:36.499
It wasn't what we often think
about in conflict situations,

00:23:36.499 --> 00:23:38.915
which is that people will lose all sense

00:23:38.915 --> 00:23:42.057
of what day to day interactions look like.

00:23:42.057 --> 00:23:44.870
So it looked like a
normal market transaction

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:48.300
if you hadn't told me that
it was in a refugee camp.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:51.189
Can you say something from
your experience with filming

00:23:51.189 --> 00:23:54.930
in terms of this kind of observation

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:59.370
in terms of rules of the
camp or rules of interaction

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:01.530
during this time of...

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:02.610
Was it surprising?

00:24:02.610 --> 00:24:03.570
Was it, no, actually.

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:05.700
It's not surprising because when a group

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.770
of people get together and
they've been living somewhere

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:10.890
for five years, maybe they
need to have certain rules,

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:13.020
otherwise the whole thing
would be generated into chaos.

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:18.020
But what was surprising to you
about the situation that you

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:21.453
were not expecting when you
went into making the film?

00:24:23.869 --> 00:24:25.404
- There are lots of things.

00:24:25.404 --> 00:24:30.404
And I think every day when
I go to the camp to shoot,

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:36.360
and it's because most
of the people, I mean,

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:39.090
they don't have any
cash money and the rice

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:42.930
that they receive from the
international aid organization,

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:46.560
so they get it every day
or in a week or somehow

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:49.893
and they have this collection of rice.

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:53.790
But that's also important
and they are waiting.

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:56.370
They have nothing to do, just traumatized.

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:59.580
That's like exhausted with
their weight of trauma

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.190
and then they exchange this with the rice.

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:07.190
But on one side, they lost everything.

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:11.250
And there are sensibility,
there are sympathy.

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:14.250
Is fluctuated.

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.020
It's like, okay, I saw everything,

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:17.975
all the horrible things happened to me.

00:25:17.975 --> 00:25:21.240
Before my eyes, I saw my son got killed.

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:22.620
I saw so many,

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:25.170
how come I can survive anymore?

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:27.390
They lost the normal sensibility.

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:31.590
So every day when I go
to the camp to film,

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:35.040
then I see, I beg, I can't sleep anymore,

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:39.180
especially the mother who
was at the end you saw

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:40.950
she was crying.

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:42.720
Like she couldn't control anymore

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:44.880
and she was hugging the tree.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:48.769
And she thought I look
like I resemble her son.

00:25:48.769 --> 00:25:51.000
And it was very heartbreaking.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:52.830
I couldn't really control.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:55.020
Even my camera, I was asking myself,

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:58.619
should I roll my camera or
should I just give her a hug?

00:25:58.619 --> 00:25:59.790
I wanted to give her a hug.

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.550
But also there is a cultural problem.

00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:07.550
Like you cannot hug a woman
in that corner of the world

00:26:07.981 --> 00:26:10.440
somehow if you are a stranger.

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:14.250
But it's like, it feels
my heart was burning

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:17.670
when I was encountering
and was interviewing her.

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:18.510
It's not an interview,

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:21.720
I just ask her what's the story?

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:23.973
And also every day I meet them.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:29.520
I didn't keep a profile as a filmmaker.

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:31.710
I went there, I didn't
use any crew member.

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:36.710
I was alone with a little
camera with a Canon 5D Mark 2

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:39.390
and I put a microphone on the camera.

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:40.770
So people are not afraid

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:44.220
and they don't have any
pressure to impress me

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.950
to like, okay, this is the
fourth television for the film.

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:47.783
No.

00:26:47.783 --> 00:26:51.180
So it was very natural and I
wanted to go under the surface.

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:54.207
I wanted to feel their pain in my camera

00:26:54.207 --> 00:26:56.220
and also personally.

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:57.840
And it's shocking every day.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:26:59.820
I couldn't sleep for a long time

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:02.820
and I was also having tough time.

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:06.230
Then it was those kids
that were a massive source

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:08.580
of inspiration for me.

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:12.210
And I saw, I learned from
them, wow, they are doing this

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:14.310
and I have to do, I have to keep going.

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.610
Even in life we have so
many trouble happens.

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:18.840
But look at these kids,

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:21.300
they have nothing but
there is still laughing,

00:27:21.300 --> 00:27:23.090
there is still moving forward.

00:27:23.090 --> 00:27:27.607
So I get inspired out of their work,

00:27:27.607 --> 00:27:31.440
how they work, how they
live there every day.

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:32.966
And there are several characters.

00:27:32.966 --> 00:27:37.170
One guy who was selling,

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:38.940
he was selling something,

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:41.773
I think vegetables and he sold everything

00:27:41.773 --> 00:27:46.080
and he tried to buy some
balloons for his kids

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:48.060
and he couldn't afford that.

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:51.930
He tried to bargain something
and he couldn't afford.

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:53.340
And the balloon sellers,

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:56.280
they're also tough 'cause
they're also selling balloons

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:59.230
and also, they have to
buy it with the money.

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:03.210
They don't have enough money
to be kind to this guy.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:04.770
And they're also very harsh

00:28:04.770 --> 00:28:07.770
and they're just like, "No,
we cannot make it for you."

00:28:07.770 --> 00:28:12.720
And they are gone and
this guy is just waiting

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:14.353
and just looking to the balloon.

00:28:14.353 --> 00:28:16.679
I was like, oh my god.

00:28:16.679 --> 00:28:19.380
And he came and he was counting the money.

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:22.920
And so, every day there are other women

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:26.370
who was saying how they
got raped, their wounds.

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:29.280
But it is too much for the audience,

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:32.310
for international audience to show this.

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:35.941
But personally, it was
very shocking for me.

00:28:35.941 --> 00:28:38.910
- Yeah, and it's interesting that you say

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:41.370
it's too much for the
international audience

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.380
to be shown, right?

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:46.170
Because it is actually reality.

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:47.970
And here we are actually saying,

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:50.989
well, reality might be
too harsh for those of us

00:28:50.989 --> 00:28:54.540
who are fortunate and lucky
to escape that reality.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:59.070
So in some ways, it's actually
worse than I hear you saying,

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:02.367
it's much, much worse
than what can be shown

00:29:02.367 --> 00:29:04.350
and what has been shown.

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:07.410
And I do wanna make a point
about what you talked about,

00:29:07.410 --> 00:29:09.131
which is the resilience.

00:29:09.131 --> 00:29:13.140
I noticed that scene as
well and it was striking

00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:15.060
for two reasons.

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:20.060
One is, as you said, like a
normal market transaction,

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:22.440
you might work very hard and then

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:24.480
not be able to afford something, right?

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:27.900
We see this issue with
deprivation all the time.

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:29.790
Those of us who are fortunate enough,

00:29:29.790 --> 00:29:31.590
we still feel deprived with,

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.510
I can't afford this particular thing.

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:34.920
But it's even worse.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:38.276
And it's probably even more exacerbated

00:29:38.276 --> 00:29:41.520
in a conflict and humanitarian situation

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:43.500
because you've really lost everything.

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:45.488
And time and time again,

00:29:45.488 --> 00:29:47.602
there were people in the film who talked

00:29:47.602 --> 00:29:52.080
about how much they had
lost and really that.

00:29:52.080 --> 00:29:54.097
And there was the scene
I think right after that

00:29:54.097 --> 00:29:55.657
where the person says,

00:29:55.657 --> 00:29:57.657
"We should just buy
balloons for the children

00:29:57.657 --> 00:30:00.973
because they should just be happy."

00:30:00.973 --> 00:30:05.400
And I think that's really
interesting because

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:08.490
in normal situations we teach
children to wait for things

00:30:08.490 --> 00:30:11.400
and to sort of have self control.

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:13.200
But in this situation,

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:16.320
how can you really deny whatever
little happiness you get

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:18.480
when life is uncertain?

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:21.390
I do think there was
another scene similarly

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:22.327
where someone said,

00:30:22.327 --> 00:30:25.170
"We just pretend we are
on holiday in this camp

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:27.990
because we don't want to
believe that this is reality."

00:30:27.990 --> 00:30:29.715
And it's really,

00:30:29.715 --> 00:30:32.850
there is a lot of work
to be done, I think,

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:35.347
to understand mental health consequences

00:30:35.347 --> 00:30:38.678
and how people cope with
this kind of situation

00:30:38.678 --> 00:30:40.980
because it's not just a situation of loss,

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:43.170
it's a situation of uncertainty.

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:45.784
And we know that in times of uncertainty,

00:30:45.784 --> 00:30:47.940
people cope in very different ways.

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:52.290
So just as we are discussing
what is it that we need

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:54.870
to know, I think that certainly
there were a few places

00:30:54.870 --> 00:30:57.769
that I felt, oh, we
should really understand

00:30:57.769 --> 00:31:01.800
what the consequences of
being in the camp are,

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:05.853
the uncertainty sort of
idea that I lost something.

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:07.890
At the end of the movie,

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:10.936
you talked about sort of
the current situation.

00:31:10.936 --> 00:31:13.320
When exactly did you film this?

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:16.023
Because you talked about
Covid 19 actually impacting

00:31:16.023 --> 00:31:17.640
the situation.

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:19.800
Can you talk a little bit about that

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:23.670
and if you've gone back or
heard anything since then,

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:26.250
what the situation has been like?

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:29.700
- Okay, so I filmed
right after the genocide

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:30.900
when they're coming.

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:33.194
So it was in 2017.

00:31:33.194 --> 00:31:36.660
So I kept in touch, but after filming,

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:40.350
I was there for three months
then I came back to Portugal

00:31:40.350 --> 00:31:41.430
and I was editing.

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:46.230
But in my postproduction
time, I had to put this,

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:49.230
okay, the latter situation
was really happening

00:31:49.230 --> 00:31:52.740
and then we see the trial

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:56.040
in the International Court
of Justice, ICJ in the Hague,

00:31:56.040 --> 00:31:58.410
that also this Covid happening.

00:31:58.410 --> 00:31:59.730
Then there was no hope.

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:03.090
And I was still in contact
with them. What's happening?

00:32:03.090 --> 00:32:04.830
Your father is back?
- "No."

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:06.749
- And there there is no hope.

00:32:06.749 --> 00:32:10.500
And slowly they were
getting out of the headline

00:32:10.500 --> 00:32:12.600
and they are no more headline

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:15.420
and then nobody is talking about that.

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:17.910
So now is terrible.

00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:20.940
It's also hard for me to have the access

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:24.330
because there is no mobile phone,

00:32:24.330 --> 00:32:26.760
no network inside the camp.

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:31.760
So I couldn't really continue
to keep in touch with them.

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:36.031
But I know some of my
friends who are working there

00:32:36.031 --> 00:32:40.170
and also some of the
refugee Rohingya people.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:42.000
So I'm in touch with them

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:43.950
and I'm asking what's really happening

00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:47.010
and they keep me updated
about the situation,

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:51.390
which is really is also
shocking because every day

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:52.800
is getting less.

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:54.690
They don't have any more education,

00:32:54.690 --> 00:32:55.650
right of education.

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:56.740
So they don't really.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:59.670
Before there was some sort of education

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:01.650
and now it's getting more worse.

00:33:01.650 --> 00:33:05.220
And so, the aid is getting less every day

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:07.080
that there is no education.

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:10.980
And also that the people, they
are getting more desperate.

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:14.670
So they are escaping from
the camp by illegal ways.

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:19.670
So sometimes, we see the news
and they died in the river

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:21.864
because they were trying to escape it,

00:33:21.864 --> 00:33:23.580
they were trying to go somewhere Indonesia

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:27.120
or Malaysia or India and it's horrible.

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:30.540
And they know that what's
really consequences coming

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:32.730
for in their life, but they don't care.

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:35.297
And they said like, "What
else do we have to do?"

00:33:35.297 --> 00:33:36.900
'Cause when I was filming,

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:41.220
I couldn't put every
statement, every testimony,

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:43.710
but there were people who
are dreaming that one day

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.710
we go back and I get
married, one day we go.

00:33:46.710 --> 00:33:47.887
The children were there saying,

00:33:47.887 --> 00:33:50.010
"What's happening with the mango trees?"

00:33:50.010 --> 00:33:51.900
And that's another shocking scene for me.

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:53.700
When the children, they were...

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:57.990
I was filming something else
and suddenly I saw them,

00:33:57.990 --> 00:33:58.990
they're having this,

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:03.813
the situation is, they had hope.

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:07.860
Even after the genocide when they came

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:11.250
and they were still
expecting they will go back,

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:13.537
international community will help

00:34:13.537 --> 00:34:16.143
to secure their basic human rights

00:34:16.143 --> 00:34:21.125
and also citizenship
rights to go back there,

00:34:21.125 --> 00:34:23.913
and before the repatriation.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:28.140
Sometimes there was something like,

00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:31.620
one time, I remember through the UN,

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:33.682
they tried to make a repatriation

00:34:33.682 --> 00:34:36.240
for a small amount of people,

00:34:36.240 --> 00:34:41.100
but they said, "We prefer
to get buried with funeral

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:44.640
rather than just going there,
getting killed and get buried.

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:46.806
And if we stay here, we get funeral.

00:34:46.806 --> 00:34:49.800
If you go there, we would not get there."

00:34:49.800 --> 00:34:51.350
So that's very,

00:34:51.350 --> 00:34:53.250
they care about these things.

00:34:53.250 --> 00:34:56.280
And the things, what's
happening in Myanmar right now

00:34:56.280 --> 00:35:00.570
also not very pleasing and is
also still ongoing genocide.

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:04.740
Yesterday I saw a video
clip from a Rohingya person.

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:06.247
He was also saying like,

00:35:06.247 --> 00:35:07.920
"Look, we are not happy.

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:12.210
We are in here stuck
in the Maungdaw region

00:35:12.210 --> 00:35:16.317
in the Rakhine State and is
still another ongoing genocide."

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.920
So international community, he was asking

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:24.870
to give some attention to
them and try to help them.

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:26.850
- Yeah, and I do want to add here

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:29.680
that neither Sohel nor I are experts

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:32.490
in international humanitarian law,

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:37.490
but on March 21st, 2022,
following a rigorous factual

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:40.080
and legal analysis, the Secretary of State

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:41.842
of the United States determined

00:35:41.842 --> 00:35:44.910
that members of the Burmese
military committed genocide

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:47.790
and crimes against
humanity against Rohingya.

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:50.790
And the United States has
concluded only seven other times

00:35:50.790 --> 00:35:53.220
since the Holocaust
genocide was committed.

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:55.230
So this determination was the eighth.

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:59.970
So there are various terms that are used

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:04.970
and various ways to classify
atrocities against humanity.

00:36:06.600 --> 00:36:09.510
But just wanted to put that out there.

00:36:09.510 --> 00:36:13.147
I think one of the related
questions Paul Sarno asked is,

00:36:13.147 --> 00:36:15.900
"Do the camp residents
know about the lawsuit

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:18.540
before the ICJ and all the other suits?

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:21.660
What are they aware of
taking from Paul's question?

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:24.510
What are they actually aware
of what the outside world is,

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:26.817
is thinking or doing in this regard?"

00:36:29.776 --> 00:36:34.776
- Well, some people know,
some people following that,

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:40.580
but most of the people that
they don't have proper education

00:36:41.040 --> 00:36:44.310
or academic education for a long time.

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:46.500
Because it is a slow poisoning.

00:36:46.500 --> 00:36:49.440
For a long time when
the Myanmar government

00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:51.990
had taken out the citizenship right

00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:54.780
and they didn't have access to the school,

00:36:54.780 --> 00:36:57.720
maybe they finish primary
school or elementary school,

00:36:57.720 --> 00:37:00.660
then they didn't have
access to the university,

00:37:00.660 --> 00:37:03.570
so they couldn't really
continue their education.

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:04.920
So what's really happening,

00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:10.110
most of them are either
fishermen or just farmer

00:37:10.110 --> 00:37:13.560
or doing something else
to make their living.

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:15.690
And now is more.

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:18.210
They don't have that fresh air anymore.

00:37:18.210 --> 00:37:20.217
They are like in an open air prison.

00:37:20.217 --> 00:37:24.090
And it is different circumstances

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:26.910
and they are not very aware

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:29.340
like in terms of general Rohingya.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:33.240
But there are some people who
are connected with network

00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:36.840
or somehow they are
they working for an NGO.

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:39.630
So they have sort of knowledge,

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:42.180
what's really happening about the lawsuit

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:46.067
and with the the ICJ and
sometimes they celebrate

00:37:46.067 --> 00:37:48.420
that The Gambia was helping,

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:50.850
that The Gambia was standing for them.

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:54.150
So it goes viral in the refugee camp.

00:37:54.150 --> 00:37:55.016
So everybody knows that,

00:37:55.016 --> 00:38:00.016
but not really like knowing
about law and the rights,

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:03.180
not everybody because, yeah.

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:04.650
I can't say about everybody,

00:38:04.650 --> 00:38:07.443
but maybe there are some changes now.

00:38:08.460 --> 00:38:09.293
- Yeah.

00:38:09.293 --> 00:38:13.170
No, and if any of the audience members

00:38:13.170 --> 00:38:17.123
has much more in depth
knowledge of humanitarian

00:38:17.123 --> 00:38:18.720
or international law,

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:21.840
please feel free to actually
share that knowledge

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.353
on the chat.

00:38:23.353 --> 00:38:26.669
Paul also asked a related, I
think quite difficult question,

00:38:26.669 --> 00:38:30.180
which is it's clear that it's
unlikely they will be able

00:38:30.180 --> 00:38:32.010
to go back to Myanmar.

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:34.131
If that is correct, which
countries do you think

00:38:34.131 --> 00:38:35.430
that they will go go?

00:38:35.430 --> 00:38:37.200
Or do we have any idea?

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:39.362
And just to put this in perspective,

00:38:39.362 --> 00:38:43.020
the statistic that actually
shocked me was to learn

00:38:43.020 --> 00:38:45.630
that there were almost a million refugees.

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:48.480
And I looked at the population
of New Haven, Connecticut,

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:51.390
where Yale Universities
are at 130,000 people

00:38:51.390 --> 00:38:52.350
in all of New Haven.

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:55.200
So this is kind of like
eight times the population

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:56.744
of New Haven.

00:38:56.744 --> 00:39:00.720
And New Haven is the
third largest city or town

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:02.738
in Connecticut in one of the states.

00:39:02.738 --> 00:39:07.710
So that just puts into
perspective just how big this,

00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:10.659
it's not a question of
a few hundred refugees.

00:39:10.659 --> 00:39:14.640
And I think very similar to
other long longstanding camps,

00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:15.930
that is Dadaab, et cetera.

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:19.260
The question I think is,
where would they go, right?

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:21.000
I understand they've been effectively,

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:22.978
they're stateless in that sense.

00:39:22.978 --> 00:39:25.650
They're not recognized as citizens.

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:27.000
I think the big question is,

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:31.380
is there any sort of
update or understanding

00:39:31.380 --> 00:39:33.903
or negotiation about where they would go?

00:39:35.705 --> 00:39:38.967
- Okay, so I just followed some news

00:39:38.967 --> 00:39:41.850
and there was a foreign
minister of Bangladesh

00:39:41.850 --> 00:39:46.850
who was trying to talk
with China, with India,

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:51.600
because this is also going
to be a regional threat.

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:52.590
What is happening?

00:39:52.590 --> 00:39:56.040
This refugee youngsters,
they are growing up

00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:58.440
and there is lots of children of,

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:02.703
I think it's 30,000 children every year.

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:08.370
I forgot the exact number,
but it's something like that.

00:40:08.370 --> 00:40:10.410
And so, what's really happening?

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:11.880
And is going to be,

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:13.890
if you're not providing education,

00:40:13.890 --> 00:40:17.458
if you're not providing
any basic human rights

00:40:17.458 --> 00:40:21.210
and everything is getting
like more smaller,

00:40:21.210 --> 00:40:24.433
more worse, then they
would get more desperate.

00:40:24.433 --> 00:40:28.061
And they will be used by other criminals,

00:40:28.061 --> 00:40:31.380
for example, especially for the drugs.

00:40:31.380 --> 00:40:36.380
And that area is very
famous for the drugs dealing

00:40:36.750 --> 00:40:38.834
because of the river, because of the sea.

00:40:38.834 --> 00:40:41.100
And there was something happening,

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:42.750
and it is not just for the Bangladesh,

00:40:42.750 --> 00:40:47.730
but also is a regional threat
for India, for Myanmar,

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:49.170
for Bangladesh, for Thailand,

00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:52.860
for other countries who
are sharing the same area.

00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:55.740
And that's why they have
like all the government,

00:40:55.740 --> 00:40:58.500
they should really take
the necessary steps

00:40:58.500 --> 00:41:02.370
or very like useful
things, not just talking.

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:07.370
And last on 25th or 24th of August,

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:13.290
I was sharing a panel with a conversation

00:41:13.290 --> 00:41:15.870
with the foreign minister of Malaysia.

00:41:15.870 --> 00:41:20.870
He also said they are very
welcoming to Rohingya refugee,

00:41:21.450 --> 00:41:24.447
but already they have some people there,

00:41:24.447 --> 00:41:25.620
the Rohingya community,

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:27.360
a big community of Rohingya.

00:41:27.360 --> 00:41:29.070
They're living in Malaysia.

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:30.780
So far, what I talk to the...

00:41:30.780 --> 00:41:33.633
Also in India, also in Indonesia,

00:41:34.770 --> 00:41:38.100
there are few numbers
and I know some people

00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:39.480
who are living in the U.S.

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:41.303
but not that big amount.

00:41:41.303 --> 00:41:44.211
It's not that effective.

00:41:44.211 --> 00:41:45.669
- Yeah.

00:41:45.669 --> 00:41:47.403
- What concern?

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:51.990
If that is correct,

00:41:51.990 --> 00:41:54.933
to what countries do
you think they will go?

00:41:55.978 --> 00:41:59.193
They still prefer to go to Myanmar.

00:42:00.044 --> 00:42:03.084
They are just not refugees.

00:42:03.084 --> 00:42:07.530
They are a group of people
with past, with honor,

00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:10.710
with culture.

00:42:10.710 --> 00:42:12.360
And now they lost everything.

00:42:12.360 --> 00:42:16.650
And there there are still saying that.

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:21.067
The people there, there are
people who are saying like,

00:42:21.067 --> 00:42:22.800
"We are just socializing.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:26.310
We feel like we have to feel
like we are on holiday."

00:42:26.310 --> 00:42:29.250
That guy after the interview,

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:31.987
he was full of tears because he said,

00:42:31.987 --> 00:42:35.190
"I had too many Honda, motor bikes,

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:37.740
and I was a very rich person,

00:42:37.740 --> 00:42:39.210
I was a very respected person.

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:40.682
Now I have nothing to do.

00:42:40.682 --> 00:42:43.110
I lost everything.

00:42:43.110 --> 00:42:44.870
- And I think...
- Yeah.

00:42:44.870 --> 00:42:47.250
- Sorry, I didn't mean to interrupt you,

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:52.250
but I just wanted to say that
I don't think we often realize

00:42:52.830 --> 00:42:56.850
that refugees in humanitarian
camps can't work.

00:42:56.850 --> 00:42:57.800
They're not allowed to work.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:43:01.080
So this is not like a migration
scenario where people go

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:03.120
to another country and they can work.

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:05.340
Even now I think the
government is deciding

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.140
whether they can work
and I think you referred

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:09.060
to it in the drug violence.

00:43:09.060 --> 00:43:11.730
And again, the way people
are coping with it is to say,

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:14.250
we are here on a holiday,
we are socializing.

00:43:14.250 --> 00:43:17.040
But in addition to income,

00:43:17.040 --> 00:43:19.350
there's also a loss of identity

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:22.500
if people get a lot of
their meaning in life

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:23.333
from working.

00:43:23.333 --> 00:43:25.138
And if they just can't work,

00:43:25.138 --> 00:43:28.260
they can obviously get
desperate for income,

00:43:28.260 --> 00:43:30.691
but also a lot of their
sense of what it means

00:43:30.691 --> 00:43:34.632
to be a useful person in society

00:43:34.632 --> 00:43:36.660
because that's the way we've always talked

00:43:36.660 --> 00:43:37.590
about people, right?

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:39.060
That you should work for a living.

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:39.997
And when you can't do that,

00:43:39.997 --> 00:43:42.300
there are a lot of issues that I think

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:43.950
will come to the fore.

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:47.407
You're completely right
in terms of these effects

00:43:47.407 --> 00:43:52.230
of violence and other sort of abuses

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:54.630
because people are just
not allowed to work.

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:57.150
I mean, imagine if any
of us were told tomorrow,

00:43:57.150 --> 00:43:59.280
I think we'd be happy
for maybe a day or two

00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:01.020
and then we'd start getting restless.

00:44:01.020 --> 00:44:03.630
So imagine when you're told
for long periods of time

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:05.865
that your status is so uncertain,

00:44:05.865 --> 00:44:07.620
you're not allowed to work.

00:44:07.620 --> 00:44:12.210
So, yeah, I think when I
heard that person talking,

00:44:12.210 --> 00:44:13.852
you could very clearly
see that he wasn't saying

00:44:13.852 --> 00:44:15.420
it very joyfully.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:18.390
He was almost saying it in
a very resigned fashion.

00:44:18.390 --> 00:44:20.613
What can we do? We can socialize here.

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:27.574
I think a good question that comes up is,

00:44:27.574 --> 00:44:29.580
and this is a tricky question, right,

00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:32.352
because, again, this is very
different from chronic poverty

00:44:32.352 --> 00:44:36.900
in that people need to know
information about their rights.

00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:39.390
And a lot of people in the documentary

00:44:39.390 --> 00:44:40.680
also talked about their rights.

00:44:40.680 --> 00:44:44.254
They talked about getting
back their rights.

00:44:44.254 --> 00:44:47.217
So did the NGOs that help in the Rohingya

00:44:47.217 --> 00:44:50.250
and the camps disseminate
information about rights

00:44:50.250 --> 00:44:53.190
or do they support them
on more of the healthcare

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:55.138
and other basic necessities?

00:44:55.138 --> 00:45:00.120
Do you see more of the
humanitarian agencies

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:02.100
have set up and there are different areas

00:45:02.100 --> 00:45:05.146
that they help in
education and healthcare?

00:45:05.146 --> 00:45:08.738
Is there a way that people are
educated about their rights?

00:45:08.738 --> 00:45:12.453
So you think that's not
yet happening in the camps?

00:45:14.880 --> 00:45:18.450
- Well, so far, I know
with my little knowledge,

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:21.120
I think there are some organizations

00:45:21.120 --> 00:45:23.310
who are working for that.

00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:26.793
But this is not really enough for this.

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:31.650
Formally, schools are closed,

00:45:31.650 --> 00:45:33.630
so there is no even school.

00:45:33.630 --> 00:45:35.490
So it is getting,

00:45:35.490 --> 00:45:38.550
because Bangladesh has
this, what I learned,

00:45:38.550 --> 00:45:41.153
they're saying like, is a refugee camp.

00:45:41.153 --> 00:45:44.467
We can't give them like a home,

00:45:44.467 --> 00:45:48.210
like giving the education and everything.

00:45:48.210 --> 00:45:51.240
So they will stay longer.
They would not go back home.

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:54.900
So that's what I realized
through the different kind

00:45:54.900 --> 00:45:57.180
of explanation from a different corner.

00:45:57.180 --> 00:46:00.000
But what I feel is very important

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:04.020
to let them know their basic rights,

00:46:04.020 --> 00:46:07.792
information about health,
about the birth control

00:46:07.792 --> 00:46:10.500
because is also like really happening.

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:12.810
And they said, like if God creates,

00:46:12.810 --> 00:46:14.770
there are also misconceptions

00:46:17.314 --> 00:46:20.070
because of their lack of knowledge,

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:23.250
they depend on something
like spiritual power,

00:46:23.250 --> 00:46:24.513
something like gods.

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:27.960
Mostly they are devoted to the religion,

00:46:27.960 --> 00:46:29.908
but at the same time,

00:46:29.908 --> 00:46:33.840
they have no any kind of material

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:36.720
or any other education.

00:46:36.720 --> 00:46:38.787
Please, don't get offended, other people.

00:46:38.787 --> 00:46:40.153
I'm not against religion,

00:46:40.153 --> 00:46:45.153
but I'm saying that
they have a saying that,

00:46:45.427 --> 00:46:48.390
"Oh, okay, if God creates the children,

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:50.007
God will take care of them."

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:54.570
But at the end of the day, you have to go,

00:46:54.570 --> 00:46:57.123
you have to ask for
food for your children.

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:04.201
They have misconception
about so many things,

00:47:04.201 --> 00:47:09.201
about way of living, about their health,

00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:12.573
the other system, other disease.

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:17.400
And I think, yeah, of
course the government,

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:19.080
Bangladesh government at the same time,

00:47:19.080 --> 00:47:21.990
some other NGO or the organization,

00:47:21.990 --> 00:47:24.003
they should take care of it more.

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:29.393
- Sorry, I was on mute.

00:47:29.393 --> 00:47:30.866
There's a good point to think about,

00:47:30.866 --> 00:47:35.430
which is that it's not just
about providing the health

00:47:35.430 --> 00:47:39.690
and the basic necessities, but, again,

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:41.130
as we talked about work,

00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:43.620
work is not just about
the money that you get,

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:46.710
it's also your chance to be
a sort of productive member

00:47:46.710 --> 00:47:48.120
of the society.

00:47:48.120 --> 00:47:49.800
So it contributes to your identity.

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:53.340
Similarly, it's not only
about giving people healthcare

00:47:53.340 --> 00:47:55.124
and basic necessities,

00:47:55.124 --> 00:47:57.720
but also telling people
about their rights, right?

00:47:57.720 --> 00:47:59.640
Treating them like equal human beings

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:02.640
and really sort of
recognizing their dignity

00:48:02.640 --> 00:48:04.140
even in this situation.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:06.690
So it's really important.

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:09.170
Sometimes we can forget
that when we are so...

00:48:12.510 --> 00:48:15.180
All of us who are in service,
of course, we'll forget it

00:48:15.180 --> 00:48:18.720
because we think, oh, if
we give give people help,

00:48:18.720 --> 00:48:19.560
then that's enough.

00:48:19.560 --> 00:48:22.130
But we forget that people are people,

00:48:22.130 --> 00:48:23.400
we are full human beings.

00:48:23.400 --> 00:48:26.250
We want our dignity to be
affirmed in whatever we do.

00:48:26.250 --> 00:48:28.530
And that is the ability to work,

00:48:28.530 --> 00:48:32.931
the ability to have freedom,
to move, to know our rights,

00:48:32.931 --> 00:48:34.498
to fight for our rights.

00:48:34.498 --> 00:48:36.697
And however, people might say,

00:48:36.697 --> 00:48:38.970
"Well, why don't you compromise or adjust?

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:40.440
If I feel very strongly,

00:48:40.440 --> 00:48:42.480
I should be allowed to express that."

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:45.060
So I think that came up a little bit

00:48:45.060 --> 00:48:46.961
because I was surprised.

00:48:46.961 --> 00:48:49.221
Not surprised, but I think
it was really reflective

00:48:49.221 --> 00:48:52.140
that despite all of what they've seen,

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:55.080
they still talked about,
"We want our rights.

00:48:55.080 --> 00:48:57.120
We want to go back and we want our rights,

00:48:57.120 --> 00:48:58.950
otherwise there's no point in going back."

00:48:58.950 --> 00:49:02.197
So they were not prepared to
just go back just like that.

00:49:02.197 --> 00:49:05.460
They said, "Only if our full
rights are restored to us."

00:49:05.460 --> 00:49:08.428
CC had a question about
what the mother was doing

00:49:08.428 --> 00:49:10.770
while the kids sold ice cream.

00:49:10.770 --> 00:49:13.426
Do you know for the kids who were selling?

00:49:13.426 --> 00:49:14.259
- Age.

00:49:16.560 --> 00:49:18.613
- She mentioned a younger child.

00:49:18.613 --> 00:49:21.090
Was there a younger child also?

00:49:21.090 --> 00:49:22.710
I don't think we saw the younger child,

00:49:22.710 --> 00:49:25.203
but you mentioned towards
the end, I wasn't sure.

00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:27.503
- Okay.

00:49:27.503 --> 00:49:31.290
So I was also wondering
what she was really doing

00:49:31.290 --> 00:49:32.910
and then I asked her,

00:49:32.910 --> 00:49:36.060
but she said she takes
care of them and she tried

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:38.400
to get some work, but
she didn't get any work.

00:49:38.400 --> 00:49:41.700
So she completely depends on income

00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:45.633
of her kids earning every day.

00:49:46.745 --> 00:49:48.990
And she takes care of other children.

00:49:48.990 --> 00:49:51.510
You see like one, two, three, four.

00:49:51.510 --> 00:49:54.990
Four of them and she
has more kids, two more.

00:49:54.990 --> 00:49:56.580
- Yeah. And they are not there.

00:49:56.580 --> 00:50:00.843
So, yeah, I mean, is most of
the women, they are like that.

00:50:01.929 --> 00:50:04.380
- And I think again, it's another thing

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:07.020
when we talk about
setting up refugee camps.

00:50:07.020 --> 00:50:09.180
It's not as simple as
giving people some housing

00:50:09.180 --> 00:50:10.350
and giving them some food.

00:50:10.350 --> 00:50:12.990
What happens is societal inequalities,

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:15.480
sort of also the same
thing happens, right?

00:50:15.480 --> 00:50:20.104
So if women have not been
used to sort of earning

00:50:20.104 --> 00:50:22.617
their own living or
doing something or people

00:50:22.617 --> 00:50:24.270
are not used to employing them,

00:50:24.270 --> 00:50:25.311
they're not going to employ them

00:50:25.311 --> 00:50:27.660
just because it's now a refugee camp.

00:50:27.660 --> 00:50:28.920
So we've gotta think about,

00:50:28.920 --> 00:50:31.800
what were they doing before?

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:34.927
What are people's social
norms about what women can

00:50:34.927 --> 00:50:38.643
and can't do and what needs to be done?

00:50:38.643 --> 00:50:43.050
And in this context, I
wanna say that at Y-RISE,

00:50:43.050 --> 00:50:46.350
we are conducting some
research in Cox's Bazar

00:50:46.350 --> 00:50:49.383
with the World Bank
and other institutions.

00:50:50.334 --> 00:50:52.410
And one of the things is
to understand violence

00:50:52.410 --> 00:50:54.120
against women.

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:55.950
Understand, someone talked about,

00:50:55.950 --> 00:50:56.783
I think she should talked

00:50:56.783 --> 00:51:00.600
about how inequalities
impact children's life.

00:51:00.600 --> 00:51:01.739
I think we are trying to understand

00:51:01.739 --> 00:51:04.621
how such conditions impact things

00:51:04.621 --> 00:51:06.900
like violence against women,

00:51:06.900 --> 00:51:09.210
which is, is it worse?

00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:12.230
Is it better because everyone's
now in a shared situation?

00:51:12.230 --> 00:51:15.480
Is it worse because there's
so much frustration?

00:51:15.480 --> 00:51:19.890
Has it become worse as people
are there for a longer time?

00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:20.850
Because if you have to do...

00:51:20.850 --> 00:51:24.287
Is it different from
violence against women

00:51:24.287 --> 00:51:27.587
in chronic poverty situations
or there's something

00:51:27.587 --> 00:51:29.854
about this particular
situation that's different

00:51:29.854 --> 00:51:31.466
because only if we understand that,

00:51:31.466 --> 00:51:34.620
we can understand what's happening.

00:51:34.620 --> 00:51:36.360
I do wanna encourage the audience

00:51:36.360 --> 00:51:40.500
to post any more questions in the Q&amp;A.

00:51:40.500 --> 00:51:45.500
And also maybe in terms of at
least my final question maybe

00:51:46.260 --> 00:51:48.603
to Sohel, but also to
the audience, which is,

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:51.810
as a filmmaker, what's their expectation

00:51:51.810 --> 00:51:54.870
of centers of learning like Yale

00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:59.870
and the university can actually
do in this giant problem?

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:03.930
We are not governments and we are not NGOs

00:52:03.930 --> 00:52:06.210
who are directly going to be there,

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:10.203
but there is a role that
universities can actually play

00:52:10.203 --> 00:52:15.203
in in maybe getting attention
to these atrocities.

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:17.250
What do you see as sort of the role?

00:52:17.250 --> 00:52:18.570
And very similar, I guess,

00:52:18.570 --> 00:52:23.036
what's your role as a
filmmaker in highlighting

00:52:23.036 --> 00:52:26.583
some of these things so that
others have awareness of it?

00:52:28.860 --> 00:52:32.490
- Okay, so let's see if
there is some audience

00:52:32.490 --> 00:52:33.323
that says something.

00:52:33.323 --> 00:52:34.620
I would love some audience reactions.

00:52:34.620 --> 00:52:38.371
What do you think is the role
of universities like Yale

00:52:38.371 --> 00:52:39.993
in terms of this?

00:52:40.830 --> 00:52:43.440
You can put it in the Q&amp;A
and I'll just pick it up

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:44.273
from there,

00:52:44.273 --> 00:52:46.375
but what do you think is
the role of universities

00:52:46.375 --> 00:52:50.520
in humanitarian crises like this?

00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:53.603
What can we do?

00:52:53.603 --> 00:52:56.100
- I can't tell you like
what you should do,

00:52:56.100 --> 00:52:59.490
but I can tell you my expectation.

00:52:59.490 --> 00:53:00.323
- Yes.

00:53:00.323 --> 00:53:01.290
- Maybe you are the right person.

00:53:01.290 --> 00:53:02.160
What you can do,

00:53:02.160 --> 00:53:05.789
you can tell about this from
the university perspective

00:53:05.789 --> 00:53:08.430
or from the academic perspective.

00:53:08.430 --> 00:53:13.170
But like if you go with the anthropology,

00:53:13.170 --> 00:53:16.650
like Malinowski went for the field work,

00:53:16.650 --> 00:53:19.110
so he brought something we didn't know,

00:53:19.110 --> 00:53:20.610
but then we started.

00:53:20.610 --> 00:53:22.500
That from this amateur anthropology,

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:24.060
then it breaks and this.

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:25.263
But I'm saying,

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:31.290
kind of a university, a
center, a research center,

00:53:31.290 --> 00:53:33.427
a university, if they talk about this,

00:53:33.427 --> 00:53:36.030
it means a lot for them.

00:53:36.030 --> 00:53:37.740
For example, right now,

00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:39.540
there are people engagement?

00:53:39.540 --> 00:53:41.130
We are talking, you are talking.

00:53:41.130 --> 00:53:44.880
So is already a lot for these,

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:48.033
one of the most persecuted
communities in the world.

00:53:49.650 --> 00:53:52.347
Almost international community
has forgotten about them.

00:53:52.347 --> 00:53:54.450
And now at university,

00:53:54.450 --> 00:53:56.950
one of the top most
university in the world, Yale,

00:53:58.244 --> 00:54:00.120
and I'm really grateful and thankful

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:02.460
to you to the university.

00:54:02.460 --> 00:54:06.437
They invited my film and
screening for the big audience,

00:54:06.437 --> 00:54:08.820
especially for the academic world.

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:12.063
And I think it has a significant impact,

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:16.050
especially for making that awareness

00:54:16.050 --> 00:54:18.450
and also it is for the future

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:20.880
and future people will know that

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:24.990
and it'll help to prevent another genocide

00:54:24.990 --> 00:54:26.823
in another corner of the world.

00:54:29.580 --> 00:54:33.210
If we have some research,

00:54:33.210 --> 00:54:35.280
why it's happening, what should we do?

00:54:35.280 --> 00:54:39.150
And some suggestion, some
writing, some film, more films,

00:54:39.150 --> 00:54:40.890
more reviews, more things.

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:44.130
So I think everybody, whatever we have,

00:54:44.130 --> 00:54:46.260
the ability, we should talk about this.

00:54:46.260 --> 00:54:48.960
We should go for this,
we should do anything.

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:50.070
So that's what I feel.

00:54:50.070 --> 00:54:52.936
As a film maker, maybe I'm not,

00:54:52.936 --> 00:54:56.670
I just tried a little contribution.

00:54:56.670 --> 00:54:58.980
But what I'd be happy to see some day,

00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:01.117
that's there are lots of people that said,

00:55:01.117 --> 00:55:04.320
"Wow, this is something
what we needed to see.

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:07.410
And in France, it was in
person screening in France

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:10.770
in Toulouse when I screened
the film and some people

00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:14.880
had to leave because they couldn't digest,

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:18.180
they couldn't continue because
it's too much for them.

00:55:18.180 --> 00:55:20.400
And then some people just came to me

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:23.587
and just gave me a hug and
then started crying and saying,

00:55:23.587 --> 00:55:25.802
"Oh my God, we didn't have
any idea what's happening

00:55:25.802 --> 00:55:28.500
in that corner of the world.

00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:30.844
We just saw some news and that's it."

00:55:30.844 --> 00:55:35.490
So because the medium like
newspaper or the television

00:55:35.490 --> 00:55:36.444
or journalistic work,

00:55:36.444 --> 00:55:39.210
I appreciate lots of great journalists.

00:55:39.210 --> 00:55:40.440
They went there.

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:44.059
They did great journalism,

00:55:44.059 --> 00:55:48.240
their picture, they portrayed
the sufferings and everything.

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:52.230
But still they have
somehow they are not free.

00:55:52.230 --> 00:55:55.260
There is always like editorial
policy of the newspaper,

00:55:55.260 --> 00:55:56.130
always a policy.

00:55:56.130 --> 00:55:58.230
So university in terms of university,

00:55:58.230 --> 00:56:01.170
they have this freedom of research.

00:56:01.170 --> 00:56:05.640
They have this freedom
of raising the voice

00:56:05.640 --> 00:56:09.600
in an authentic way honest and genuinely.

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:13.320
And then, I think the university
are more in university.

00:56:13.320 --> 00:56:16.560
I'm so grateful to some
other universities.

00:56:16.560 --> 00:56:17.640
They also invited me.

00:56:17.640 --> 00:56:20.730
Almost now 15 universities
around the world,

00:56:20.730 --> 00:56:22.620
they invited me with this film.

00:56:22.620 --> 00:56:25.297
And I'm looking forward to the U.S. trip

00:56:25.297 --> 00:56:28.650
where I'm going to screen my film in UCLA,

00:56:29.484 --> 00:56:32.557
NYU and UC Berkeley.

00:56:32.557 --> 00:56:35.820
And there are lots of
universities we are talking

00:56:35.820 --> 00:56:37.080
like everyday.

00:56:37.080 --> 00:56:41.580
So that's what I, as a
filmmaker, with this film,

00:56:41.580 --> 00:56:44.460
I would like to tell this story
to the world and to feel it,

00:56:44.460 --> 00:56:47.755
not just giving the information
but to feel and to fly

00:56:47.755 --> 00:56:49.440
with this pain.

00:56:49.440 --> 00:56:50.760
That's what I want.

00:56:50.760 --> 00:56:51.593
- And I think you-

00:56:51.593 --> 00:56:53.823
- To be part of the journey.

00:56:54.930 --> 00:56:55.763
- [Neela] Thank you.

00:56:55.763 --> 00:56:59.940
I think that there is one more question.

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:02.280
I just wanna make a note
about the role of university.

00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:05.730
So we talked a little bit
about the label of genocide

00:57:05.730 --> 00:57:08.520
and when that label is applied
and when it's not applied.

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:09.780
And it might just seem that,

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:14.130
oh, we are sitting here
in uncomfortable studies

00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:17.091
and libraries and universities
and offices and deciding

00:57:17.091 --> 00:57:18.207
whether to call something.

00:57:18.207 --> 00:57:21.480
But I think labels provide
legitimacy and universities

00:57:21.480 --> 00:57:23.190
do the same thing,
which I hear you saying.

00:57:23.190 --> 00:57:24.360
They provide a platform,

00:57:24.360 --> 00:57:26.973
they provide a legitimacy
to say this is real,

00:57:28.080 --> 00:57:29.430
this is important,

00:57:29.430 --> 00:57:32.010
this is something we
should pay attention to.

00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:35.872
And I think that makes a lot in terms

00:57:35.872 --> 00:57:38.790
of moving world opinion forward.

00:57:38.790 --> 00:57:41.460
I also think your point
about freedom of research

00:57:41.460 --> 00:57:43.920
is extremely important.

00:57:43.920 --> 00:57:46.834
Universities have freedom
to research things

00:57:46.834 --> 00:57:50.493
that might be uncomfortable but need

00:57:50.493 --> 00:57:52.770
to be actually revealed.

00:57:52.770 --> 00:57:54.987
So it's a very, I think important

00:57:54.987 --> 00:57:59.970
and quite all inspiring
responsibility that we do have

00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:01.020
as a university.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:02.040
You've made the film,

00:58:02.040 --> 00:58:03.870
you've really touched
the hearts of people.

00:58:03.870 --> 00:58:06.780
You brought out the
reality as a filmmaker,

00:58:06.780 --> 00:58:10.260
you've made this reality vivid to people.

00:58:10.260 --> 00:58:14.070
Also universities are engaged
in teaching young people.

00:58:14.070 --> 00:58:19.070
I think, what do we do to
avoid the next such atrocity?

00:58:19.290 --> 00:58:20.970
What have we learned?

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:24.190
And I think in the light of discussions

00:58:24.190 --> 00:58:28.530
about the liberal arts
and learning from history,

00:58:28.530 --> 00:58:33.057
I think it really is important
to think about the role

00:58:33.057 --> 00:58:38.040
of centers of learning
in something about this.

00:58:38.040 --> 00:58:41.202
And there's one more question actually

00:58:41.202 --> 00:58:43.620
that we had about the film from CC.

00:58:43.620 --> 00:58:46.380
So I noticed that you
only shoot the upper body

00:58:46.380 --> 00:58:48.990
of the man who lost his legs.

00:58:48.990 --> 00:58:51.030
It seems that you don't
want to sell miseries.

00:58:51.030 --> 00:58:53.223
And you talked a little bit about it.

00:58:54.330 --> 00:58:55.848
Can you tell us a bit
about your thinking process

00:58:55.848 --> 00:58:57.540
of making that shot?

00:58:57.540 --> 00:58:59.610
And even I realized that and I was like,

00:58:59.610 --> 00:59:02.250
wait a minute, he doesn't
have, his legs were shot.

00:59:02.250 --> 00:59:05.550
I mean, from the angle
that you've shot him,

00:59:05.550 --> 00:59:09.270
he looks actually quite
healthy and very well to do.

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:11.261
Can you tell us a little bit
about the thinking process

00:59:11.261 --> 00:59:14.343
in terms of making the shot?

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:17.287
- Well, there are several shots like that.

00:59:17.287 --> 00:59:21.960
And then, I always try
to treat it with respect,

00:59:21.960 --> 00:59:24.021
with compassion, with respect.

00:59:24.021 --> 00:59:25.470
And I really felt it.

00:59:25.470 --> 00:59:27.208
It's not just for the cinematic journey,

00:59:27.208 --> 00:59:29.820
but also as a person.

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:30.893
And I didn't, as I'm saying,

00:59:30.893 --> 00:59:32.529
even I was having problem

00:59:32.529 --> 00:59:35.760
if I should put the mother crying,

00:59:35.760 --> 00:59:40.760
then finally I choose
to keep it in the film.

00:59:40.770 --> 00:59:43.590
And there are other more horrible shots,

00:59:43.590 --> 00:59:46.530
like really, it's too much that way

00:59:46.530 --> 00:59:48.570
for the international audience.

00:59:48.570 --> 00:59:50.880
Maybe the people who already saw there,

00:59:50.880 --> 00:59:54.937
is usual for them and for
even some people who will say,

00:59:54.937 --> 00:59:56.430
"Oh, this is nothing."

00:59:56.430 --> 00:59:57.937
If they see my film, they will say,

00:59:57.937 --> 00:59:59.010
"Oh, this is nothing,"

00:59:59.010 --> 01:00:02.280
in terms of what's really
happening to them."

01:00:02.280 --> 01:00:05.670
So I tried to have these people,

01:00:05.670 --> 01:00:10.260
and in my framing, I was
following mostly observational.

01:00:10.260 --> 01:00:14.083
And then I'm grateful to all those people

01:00:14.083 --> 01:00:16.560
who allowed me to film them.

01:00:16.560 --> 01:00:19.950
And I also didn't really
want to embarrass them

01:00:19.950 --> 01:00:21.840
in terms of doing something.

01:00:21.840 --> 01:00:24.900
And I didn't want to take disadvantages.

01:00:24.900 --> 01:00:28.320
Maybe in cinematic way, it could be great,

01:00:28.320 --> 01:00:33.320
but I left some shots
because it doesn't work

01:00:33.330 --> 01:00:36.870
in terms of ethics, in terms of aesthetic.

01:00:36.870 --> 01:00:39.360
So I tried to make like that way.

01:00:39.360 --> 01:00:40.959
And then of course, as I'm saying,

01:00:40.959 --> 01:00:44.223
it's a very tough and hard work.

01:00:45.141 --> 01:00:46.950
It is very dark.

01:00:46.950 --> 01:00:50.250
At the same time, it's
a pain, it's a tragedy,

01:00:50.250 --> 01:00:51.300
it's a loss.

01:00:51.300 --> 01:00:53.763
Then I'm like, how can I deal it?

01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:55.613
For long time,

01:00:55.613 --> 01:00:58.410
it took long time in terms of editing,

01:00:58.410 --> 01:01:01.740
but also in terms of when
I was filming every day.

01:01:01.740 --> 01:01:06.630
And I try to be very respectful
and passionate to them

01:01:06.630 --> 01:01:08.361
and they understand it because,

01:01:08.361 --> 01:01:12.390
if you see all the people
who are talking to the camera

01:01:12.390 --> 01:01:15.180
or talking to me, you see their eyes.

01:01:15.180 --> 01:01:20.180
And those eyes, they can't
lie if you see their eyes.

01:01:20.490 --> 01:01:24.180
So then I tried to make
these eyes to the audience

01:01:24.180 --> 01:01:26.368
to see their eyes.

01:01:26.368 --> 01:01:29.850
I was reading a review,

01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:33.444
an article from the Myanmar
perspective, and they said,

01:01:33.444 --> 01:01:36.321
"Oh, they're lying.
Those people are lying.

01:01:36.321 --> 01:01:40.470
Those are just a lie,
they're a false statement."

01:01:40.470 --> 01:01:41.767
And then I was really like,

01:01:41.767 --> 01:01:45.960
"No, come on. How come these
people can lie with me?

01:01:45.960 --> 01:01:49.380
And they're with full of
tears with that old man,

01:01:49.380 --> 01:01:53.013
the old man who had like two
men who were sitting together.

01:01:54.000 --> 01:01:55.350
the beard man

01:01:55.350 --> 01:01:56.670
He looks like...

01:01:56.670 --> 01:01:58.503
I lost my father.

01:01:59.610 --> 01:02:03.240
I found like he's another father of me.

01:02:03.240 --> 01:02:06.210
And when he was talking,
he lost everything.

01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:07.143
But the way,

01:02:09.330 --> 01:02:12.150
the choice of his words, intonation

01:02:12.150 --> 01:02:15.420
and everything was so
respectful, so humble.

01:02:15.420 --> 01:02:18.060
And also it shows his personality.

01:02:18.060 --> 01:02:19.226
And he lost everything.

01:02:19.226 --> 01:02:23.550
But he still, the guy is quite controlled.

01:02:23.550 --> 01:02:25.818
And I was really amazed. Oh my God!

01:02:25.818 --> 01:02:27.510
These people,

01:02:27.510 --> 01:02:30.483
how they can bear this wound inside them.

01:02:33.360 --> 01:02:35.370
- No, it's a great point you raised

01:02:35.370 --> 01:02:39.606
about how do you depict suffering

01:02:39.606 --> 01:02:43.080
without sort of exploiting
people suffering.

01:02:43.080 --> 01:02:46.320
There's been a lot of
backlash in the NGO world

01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:48.030
around what is called poverty porn,

01:02:48.030 --> 01:02:51.450
which is depicting people
in extreme suffering.

01:02:51.450 --> 01:02:53.550
And it really is a difficult choice.

01:02:53.550 --> 01:02:55.380
On the one hand, you do want to show that.

01:02:55.380 --> 01:02:57.120
You don't want to sugarcoat reality.

01:02:57.120 --> 01:02:58.383
But on the other hand,

01:03:00.030 --> 01:03:04.560
both from the perspective of
sort of keeping people's agency

01:03:04.560 --> 01:03:07.594
and also what Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie

01:03:07.594 --> 01:03:10.230
of "The Danger of a Single Story".

01:03:10.230 --> 01:03:13.140
These people are people
who have fled their homes

01:03:13.140 --> 01:03:16.650
and they are now refugees in a camp.

01:03:16.650 --> 01:03:18.240
But that's not only who they are.

01:03:18.240 --> 01:03:19.380
They're also children.

01:03:19.380 --> 01:03:21.900
They're also ice cream sellers.

01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:23.643
They're also balloon sellers.

01:03:24.526 --> 01:03:27.060
So I think you've done a great job in sort

01:03:27.060 --> 01:03:32.040
of not just focusing on only
all of the suffering or trying

01:03:32.040 --> 01:03:33.390
to exploit that.

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:35.352
I think it's a very fine
line that then you filmed,

01:03:35.352 --> 01:03:37.470
so I thought that was very well done.

01:03:37.470 --> 01:03:39.720
That was-
- Thank you, Neela.

01:03:39.720 --> 01:03:41.160
Thank you.

01:03:41.160 --> 01:03:42.150
- And yes,

01:03:42.150 --> 01:03:44.910
as Zeid Ra'ad said, the
role of the university

01:03:44.910 --> 01:03:47.250
with the help of filmmakers
is to show what is happening,

01:03:47.250 --> 01:03:49.650
to document what is actually happening.

01:03:49.650 --> 01:03:51.990
But to document it, as I said,

01:03:51.990 --> 01:03:55.830
not just in a way that shows
only one story or one part,

01:03:55.830 --> 01:03:57.090
it's all only suffering,

01:03:57.090 --> 01:04:00.240
but to show the full reality
of what is happening, right?

01:04:00.240 --> 01:04:04.830
How human beings cope in such
situations, the good, the bad,

01:04:04.830 --> 01:04:07.890
so that we can have sort
of a better response.

01:04:07.890 --> 01:04:10.678
I'm gonna throw it open if
there are other questions

01:04:10.678 --> 01:04:14.433
that our audience has or comments,

01:04:15.450 --> 01:04:18.900
and then maybe ask your final
sort of questions, Sohel,

01:04:18.900 --> 01:04:22.080
in terms of anything else
that you would like to say,

01:04:22.080 --> 01:04:23.589
given that you have this time,

01:04:23.589 --> 01:04:27.810
and this will be shown not
just to the audience right now,

01:04:27.810 --> 01:04:29.340
but to the broader audience.

01:04:29.340 --> 01:04:32.760
I think Kathy had a
question, which is, oh, yes,

01:04:32.760 --> 01:04:34.620
this was an interesting one.

01:04:34.620 --> 01:04:37.740
Have you been able to screen
the film at the camp itself?

01:04:37.740 --> 01:04:39.240
Were you able to go back and share?

01:04:39.240 --> 01:04:40.998
And also my add-on question to that is,

01:04:40.998 --> 01:04:43.530
have you been able to share
the responses to the film

01:04:43.530 --> 01:04:46.977
with the people who were in the film?

01:04:46.977 --> 01:04:47.830
- Yeah.

01:04:47.830 --> 01:04:50.010
- Well, unfortunately, not yet,

01:04:50.010 --> 01:04:51.900
but I would love to do that and tried.

01:04:51.900 --> 01:04:54.501
And now next year I'm planning to go there

01:04:54.501 --> 01:04:58.590
and to screen the film and
maybe I will follow up.

01:04:58.590 --> 01:05:00.180
I will make another film.

01:05:00.180 --> 01:05:02.523
What's really happening
after the five years,

01:05:04.113 --> 01:05:05.970
Where is Ayas and Asia?

01:05:05.970 --> 01:05:09.420
And I have to find them
because I lost their track

01:05:09.420 --> 01:05:12.660
since they're mobile because
our only medium was mobile,

01:05:12.660 --> 01:05:15.390
so I was getting touch
and then I lost them.

01:05:15.390 --> 01:05:17.820
Now I would really love
to show the film to them

01:05:17.820 --> 01:05:20.970
and also having the feedback,
what they really love.

01:05:20.970 --> 01:05:25.521
But what I can tell you,
when I was filming every day,

01:05:25.521 --> 01:05:27.150
and then at the end of the day,

01:05:27.150 --> 01:05:30.060
that all the kids, they
became like my friends

01:05:30.060 --> 01:05:31.380
and they follow me.

01:05:31.380 --> 01:05:33.840
And then I was showing
them from my camera,

01:05:33.840 --> 01:05:34.803
like, "Hey, look how you look like."

01:05:34.803 --> 01:05:37.560
And they'll, "Oh, show me. Show me more.

01:05:37.560 --> 01:05:38.393
Oh, good."

01:05:38.393 --> 01:05:42.540
So it's funny, it's
pleasing and it's so nice.

01:05:42.540 --> 01:05:44.763
I can't forget that memory.

01:05:45.720 --> 01:05:46.830
- That's great.

01:05:46.830 --> 01:05:48.338
And I hope you have a chance

01:05:48.338 --> 01:05:51.875
to actually show this more widely

01:05:51.875 --> 01:05:55.378
even to those who are
actually in the camp.

01:05:55.378 --> 01:05:57.410
I think another question
that was asked was,

01:05:57.410 --> 01:05:58.914
as documentary filmmaker,

01:05:58.914 --> 01:06:01.094
and I think this is a little
bit of what we are discussing,

01:06:01.094 --> 01:06:04.350
like how do you balance
the telling of the stories

01:06:04.350 --> 01:06:05.580
while trying to be considerate

01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:07.470
of not bringing out any trauma?

01:06:07.470 --> 01:06:09.420
So we talked about not exploiting people,

01:06:09.420 --> 01:06:12.300
but also just having
people relate their trauma

01:06:12.300 --> 01:06:13.620
can be triggering for them.

01:06:13.620 --> 01:06:14.874
I think of the mother who lost her son

01:06:14.874 --> 01:06:17.160
and the child who was
talking about how things

01:06:17.160 --> 01:06:18.840
would be better when his father was back.

01:06:18.840 --> 01:06:22.080
So how do you deal with the
situation of asking people

01:06:22.080 --> 01:06:23.241
to recount their trauma,

01:06:23.241 --> 01:06:26.205
but also sort of playing
that balancing role

01:06:26.205 --> 01:06:30.723
with making sure that you don't
bring out any more trauma?

01:06:32.250 --> 01:06:36.330
- So it's a very significant question.

01:06:36.330 --> 01:06:38.310
That's how I'm trying to evolve.

01:06:38.310 --> 01:06:41.670
I'm trying to improve
myself as a filmmaker.

01:06:41.670 --> 01:06:42.930
It's not just with the Rohingya,

01:06:42.930 --> 01:06:45.270
but if you look my other works,

01:06:45.270 --> 01:06:49.290
like I did with the two
Portuguese shepard ladies

01:06:49.290 --> 01:06:53.040
with shepard women, they
are 85 and 87 years old.

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:54.690
They work in the middle of nowhere.

01:06:54.690 --> 01:06:58.180
I'm a guy from Bangladesh
and they have never heard

01:06:58.180 --> 01:07:00.570
about the country and
they have no idea where

01:07:00.570 --> 01:07:01.680
is Bangladeshi.

01:07:01.680 --> 01:07:04.290
It's something like that for
them is a different planet,

01:07:04.290 --> 01:07:07.260
like a moon or sun or something like that.

01:07:07.260 --> 01:07:08.913
So they have a tragedy.

01:07:09.780 --> 01:07:12.720
The women, the shepherd lady, Dalina,

01:07:12.720 --> 01:07:14.520
I call her grandma.

01:07:14.520 --> 01:07:18.570
And she lost her husband,
he was also a shepherd,

01:07:18.570 --> 01:07:19.869
by a car crash.

01:07:19.869 --> 01:07:23.460
And the guy who made
the car crash crashed,

01:07:23.460 --> 01:07:27.063
he is asking money for
the damage of his car.

01:07:29.628 --> 01:07:31.830
It's such a trauma.

01:07:31.830 --> 01:07:35.400
And she moved from one
city to other city to work

01:07:35.400 --> 01:07:36.420
and all this.

01:07:36.420 --> 01:07:38.310
She was living with her husband

01:07:38.310 --> 01:07:40.950
and they were okay with shepherd,

01:07:40.950 --> 01:07:42.780
with the animals and everything,

01:07:42.780 --> 01:07:47.780
but then she has to carry this
trauma, losing her husband.

01:07:48.300 --> 01:07:51.900
And then is another pain that someone

01:07:51.900 --> 01:07:53.699
is knocking the door asking the money

01:07:53.699 --> 01:07:55.503
for the damage of his car.

01:07:57.150 --> 01:08:00.810
I asked like, okay, I
know this is the film,

01:08:00.810 --> 01:08:05.790
but I have to deal it very
smoothly, very tenderly.

01:08:05.790 --> 01:08:07.800
And then we found,

01:08:07.800 --> 01:08:10.140
I didn't want her, but she was crying.

01:08:10.140 --> 01:08:11.190
She was like,

01:08:11.190 --> 01:08:14.430
because my Portuguese back then,
my Portuguese was not good,

01:08:14.430 --> 01:08:18.043
but I was doing my
masters in 2012 and '14.

01:08:18.043 --> 01:08:21.120
I came from Belgium then to do this.

01:08:21.120 --> 01:08:24.810
That it was, because what I try to do

01:08:24.810 --> 01:08:27.480
is I tried to let them know myself

01:08:27.480 --> 01:08:30.175
and explore like, okay, there
is a universal language,

01:08:30.175 --> 01:08:31.450
there is

01:08:33.940 --> 01:08:36.697
a kind of unwritten bond

01:08:38.250 --> 01:08:40.020
between filmmaker and the character,

01:08:40.020 --> 01:08:42.670
especially when you are
doing a creative documentary.

01:08:45.064 --> 01:08:46.260
If the character,

01:08:46.260 --> 01:08:48.690
if the people who we are filming,

01:08:48.690 --> 01:08:53.280
if they have some confusion,
if they don't trust you,

01:08:53.280 --> 01:08:55.350
you would never get proper,

01:08:55.350 --> 01:08:58.040
you would never get a genuine story.

01:08:58.040 --> 01:09:00.180
and you would never be possible.

01:09:00.180 --> 01:09:04.110
It's impossible to make a
good film in terms of that.

01:09:04.110 --> 01:09:07.320
So the trauma is always
like when we deal this pain,

01:09:07.320 --> 01:09:10.800
and normally my films comes from pain,

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:14.550
my films mostly like tragedy, loss, pain,

01:09:14.550 --> 01:09:17.190
and all this kind of work is, I don't know

01:09:17.190 --> 01:09:20.970
how I go with this kind of film

01:09:20.970 --> 01:09:23.886
and I get inspired and I make it.

01:09:23.886 --> 01:09:25.803
And so with this grandma,

01:09:26.820 --> 01:09:31.410
I just gave her a situation to unfold,

01:09:31.410 --> 01:09:36.030
to reveal what's really her universe is.

01:09:36.030 --> 01:09:40.140
And then she started revealing naturally

01:09:40.140 --> 01:09:43.470
without just asking how
your husband got killed

01:09:43.470 --> 01:09:45.753
and how your loss happened.

01:09:45.753 --> 01:09:47.130
Were you very sad?

01:09:47.130 --> 01:09:49.890
And, no, I didn't ask anything.

01:09:49.890 --> 01:09:51.540
I just let her talk.

01:09:51.540 --> 01:09:55.773
And all those people when I was
filming in the refugee camp,

01:09:56.850 --> 01:09:59.809
I just said, "How are
you? How is your day?

01:09:59.809 --> 01:10:01.500
How do you feel?

01:10:01.500 --> 01:10:03.510
What are you thinking?"

01:10:03.510 --> 01:10:08.310
So it started from very a simple question.

01:10:08.310 --> 01:10:12.330
And then it linked with something else.

01:10:12.330 --> 01:10:16.675
And then when it comes out and
either they smile or they cry

01:10:16.675 --> 01:10:19.140
or they're just normal.

01:10:19.140 --> 01:10:20.913
And there is one woman,

01:10:23.101 --> 01:10:24.870
she was pregnant

01:10:24.870 --> 01:10:29.190
and then I put in one version of the film.

01:10:29.190 --> 01:10:32.580
She was pregnant and I was
asking her, "What are you doing?"

01:10:32.580 --> 01:10:36.540
She asked me to film her
and her child inside her.

01:10:36.540 --> 01:10:38.160
And she said, "Are you excited?"

01:10:38.160 --> 01:10:39.667
Yes, I'm excited."

01:10:39.667 --> 01:10:42.177
"And where is her his father?"

01:10:43.117 --> 01:10:44.337
"He was slaughtered."

01:10:45.780 --> 01:10:50.610
So the way she was
responding is like telling

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:53.190
that she had a breakfast or something.

01:10:53.190 --> 01:10:58.190
You know how these people,
this mental trauma,

01:10:58.500 --> 01:11:02.730
this loss, this tragedy made these people

01:11:02.730 --> 01:11:06.180
like this level of like, kind
of, they lost everything.

01:11:06.180 --> 01:11:07.560
They're no more human.

01:11:07.560 --> 01:11:11.160
They're not sick, but they're
live in different level

01:11:11.160 --> 01:11:16.160
of, I can't explain
properly with my words,

01:11:16.290 --> 01:11:19.770
but for me, the film is
good to explain the things.

01:11:19.770 --> 01:11:23.280
And then this is how I normally handle,

01:11:23.280 --> 01:11:25.983
and sorry that I can read the acting.

01:11:29.850 --> 01:11:34.440
- Yeah, no, it was really about
this balancing act that you

01:11:34.440 --> 01:11:37.800
have to make as a filmmaker
between telling the story

01:11:37.800 --> 01:11:39.810
and sort of figuring the trauma

01:11:39.810 --> 01:11:42.600
when people are telling you the story.

01:11:42.600 --> 01:11:43.860
- Yes.

01:11:43.860 --> 01:11:46.530
Then we go with the,

01:11:46.530 --> 01:11:50.100
like when we have food, what we see.

01:11:50.100 --> 01:11:53.013
We don't go for everything
at the same time.

01:11:54.136 --> 01:11:57.570
We choose our choice, our taste.

01:11:57.570 --> 01:12:00.750
So in the same thing, if I could put,

01:12:00.750 --> 01:12:05.750
I could put all kind of injury
wounds and more suffering,

01:12:06.450 --> 01:12:09.961
more things, it would not
be accepted as it is now.

01:12:09.961 --> 01:12:10.794
- [Neela] Yeah.

01:12:10.794 --> 01:12:13.800
- It would not touch a big community.

01:12:13.800 --> 01:12:16.200
It would not touch like,
okay, this is maybe.

01:12:16.200 --> 01:12:19.320
Maybe it could touch, but then it's like

01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:20.700
you will understand, okay,

01:12:20.700 --> 01:12:23.730
this filmmaker is just
trying to make me sad.

01:12:23.730 --> 01:12:24.660
- Yeah.

01:12:24.660 --> 01:12:26.250
- But that's not my view.

01:12:26.250 --> 01:12:29.313
That's not what I'm trying to do.

01:12:31.139 --> 01:12:33.090
- And I do want to add one more thing,

01:12:33.090 --> 01:12:35.043
which is I think that,

01:12:35.043 --> 01:12:40.043
we need to make sure that
we treat people's responses

01:12:40.485 --> 01:12:43.020
in the situation as precious, right?

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:46.500
And that's why it's important
that you've taken the trouble

01:12:46.500 --> 01:12:47.550
to build that trust.

01:12:47.550 --> 01:12:50.580
You talked about trust which
takes a long time to build

01:12:50.580 --> 01:12:53.722
and ask them questions,
very vulnerable questions.

01:12:53.722 --> 01:12:56.790
And it's our responsibility
then to share it

01:12:56.790 --> 01:12:58.284
as widely as possible

01:12:58.284 --> 01:13:01.459
so we don't have 50 people all descending

01:13:01.459 --> 01:13:03.570
on the camp with the same idea

01:13:03.570 --> 01:13:05.730
and keeping on asking the same questions

01:13:05.730 --> 01:13:06.960
over and over again.

01:13:06.960 --> 01:13:10.358
I think that if we have
people who are going in

01:13:10.358 --> 01:13:13.710
and who are sort of building trust

01:13:13.710 --> 01:13:15.210
with the communities there

01:13:15.210 --> 01:13:18.630
and who are asking these
questions and getting responses,

01:13:18.630 --> 01:13:20.100
I think we should.

01:13:20.100 --> 01:13:22.710
Our responsibilities to
share that information

01:13:22.710 --> 01:13:24.360
as much as possible.

01:13:24.360 --> 01:13:26.640
And I do wanna say,
for example, at Y-RISE,

01:13:26.640 --> 01:13:27.960
I mentioned this a little bit,

01:13:27.960 --> 01:13:31.206
but we are doing some
research collecting data

01:13:31.206 --> 01:13:34.455
around consumption and among other things

01:13:34.455 --> 01:13:37.620
and starting out with things like,

01:13:37.620 --> 01:13:40.170
what is the extent of
violence against women?

01:13:40.170 --> 01:13:42.415
But also maybe looking at
other questions and so on.

01:13:42.415 --> 01:13:44.310
And one of the things we want to do

01:13:44.310 --> 01:13:47.337
is we are gonna be in the
data collection process

01:13:47.337 --> 01:13:49.630
in October, November,

01:13:49.630 --> 01:13:54.060
have the data early next year
and share that data widely

01:13:54.060 --> 01:13:58.410
so that we don't have a
constant stream of researchers

01:13:58.410 --> 01:13:59.917
who are going in there and saying,

01:13:59.917 --> 01:14:02.280
"Oh, I must ask my question

01:14:02.280 --> 01:14:04.282
because this is a particular case

01:14:04.282 --> 01:14:07.290
where you can't keep
asking people 100 times

01:14:07.290 --> 01:14:08.190
over and over again.

01:14:08.190 --> 01:14:09.990
It's not just that people will get tired,

01:14:09.990 --> 01:14:12.900
it's also that people
don't want to keep talking

01:14:12.900 --> 01:14:14.490
about the trauma over and over again.

01:14:14.490 --> 01:14:17.920
So we should be respectful and
I think one of our big duties

01:14:17.920 --> 01:14:21.504
is to make sure that we are
not duplicating research

01:14:21.504 --> 01:14:26.504
or storytelling so that we are
respectful of people's sort

01:14:26.729 --> 01:14:29.850
of time and what they're going through

01:14:29.850 --> 01:14:32.130
when they provide answers
to these questions.

01:14:32.130 --> 01:14:34.050
So I'm glad you mentioned trust.

01:14:34.050 --> 01:14:37.146
It's a very long process
and we just wanna make sure

01:14:37.146 --> 01:14:39.090
that others don't do that.

01:14:39.090 --> 01:14:40.770
And that was a wonderful story about...

01:14:40.770 --> 01:14:43.830
Very sad about the
Portuguese lady as well.

01:14:43.830 --> 01:14:48.090
So I guess you probably have
some expertise I wanna say

01:14:48.090 --> 01:14:50.370
or some experience in these situations.

01:14:50.370 --> 01:14:52.262
And again, I think the other
main lesson I'm getting

01:14:52.262 --> 01:14:55.744
from you is, in these
difficult situations,

01:14:55.744 --> 01:14:59.850
it's always good to have
somebody who has experience

01:14:59.850 --> 01:15:01.050
in these situations.

01:15:01.050 --> 01:15:05.790
I think if we just go in as
just researchers or filmmakers

01:15:05.790 --> 01:15:07.628
who don't know anything
about the situation,

01:15:07.628 --> 01:15:09.577
never worked in similar situations,

01:15:09.577 --> 01:15:12.630
we are likely to cause
more harm than to do good.

01:15:12.630 --> 01:15:15.960
So I think it's worth it
for us to actually think

01:15:15.960 --> 01:15:16.793
about that.

01:15:16.793 --> 01:15:21.740
But I echo CC who said
that I learned so much

01:15:22.620 --> 01:15:24.570
from Sohel and the Rohingya people.

01:15:24.570 --> 01:15:27.150
I did too just watching this documentary

01:15:27.150 --> 01:15:28.650
for an hour and 15 minutes.

01:15:28.650 --> 01:15:31.800
I think I learned more
than reading everything

01:15:31.800 --> 01:15:36.660
and it really sank in
in a way that I can read

01:15:36.660 --> 01:15:39.480
any news article or whatever proclamation,

01:15:39.480 --> 01:15:41.430
but this really brings it home.

01:15:41.430 --> 01:15:43.199
And also to thank you for the chance.

01:15:43.199 --> 01:15:45.240
Let us witness the stories.

01:15:45.240 --> 01:15:49.506
I'm going to actually just
call on Teresa Delgado,

01:15:49.506 --> 01:15:54.030
who's the Head of the
Refugee Program at McMillan

01:15:54.030 --> 01:15:57.270
to say a few words because
I think this is thanks to.

01:15:57.270 --> 01:15:59.130
It was organized through the program,

01:15:59.130 --> 01:16:00.840
the Refugee Program at McMillan.

01:16:00.840 --> 01:16:04.050
And this is one of the many
things that the center does.

01:16:04.050 --> 01:16:06.875
So, Teresa, if I can have
you just say a few words

01:16:06.875 --> 01:16:09.720
and then I'll close this.

01:16:09.720 --> 01:16:10.920
And thank you so much too.

01:16:10.920 --> 01:16:11.940
I hope I have answered.

01:16:11.940 --> 01:16:13.140
If I haven't answered any

01:16:13.140 --> 01:16:14.773
or Sohel hasn't answered
any audience questions,

01:16:14.773 --> 01:16:16.350
or you haven't had a chance to ask him,

01:16:16.350 --> 01:16:19.260
can you re-just-copy it so that I know

01:16:19.260 --> 01:16:20.580
that we haven't answered it?

01:16:20.580 --> 01:16:22.320
But I think we have gone through all

01:16:22.320 --> 01:16:24.960
and thank you for being
such an engaged audience.

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:25.793
Teresa.

01:16:26.910 --> 01:16:31.467
- Yeah, no, I just want
as well to thank everybody

01:16:31.467 --> 01:16:35.490
for participating in this session.

01:16:35.490 --> 01:16:39.120
And for us, we see the role that we have

01:16:39.120 --> 01:16:43.432
as mainly sort of given the opportunity

01:16:43.432 --> 01:16:47.290
to people to see

01:16:48.180 --> 01:16:51.063
and share the experiences of others,

01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:56.760
of the refugee, of
people who have no voice.

01:16:56.760 --> 01:17:00.030
So actually we see this as a platform.

01:17:00.030 --> 01:17:02.670
And given that we are here at Yale,

01:17:02.670 --> 01:17:05.073
I think our role is just to,

01:17:07.230 --> 01:17:10.890
I mean, engage with programs

01:17:10.890 --> 01:17:14.340
as well as like the Y-RISE
in terms of the research

01:17:14.340 --> 01:17:15.300
that can be done,

01:17:15.300 --> 01:17:20.300
but balancing also with the
other type of dissemination,

01:17:21.090 --> 01:17:24.666
let's say, of showing, I
mean all of the, what Sohel

01:17:24.666 --> 01:17:28.830
was talking about, the empathy, I guess,

01:17:28.830 --> 01:17:32.610
in order to relate to the refugees

01:17:32.610 --> 01:17:37.610
and give them the rights
that have been taken aback.

01:17:39.840 --> 01:17:43.060
So hope you can continue to

01:17:46.380 --> 01:17:51.047
participate and to watch
the seminars and films.

01:17:51.047 --> 01:17:55.680
And if you have any suggestions,
please do write to us.

01:17:55.680 --> 01:17:57.090
Thank you.

01:17:57.090 --> 01:17:58.080
Okay.
- Thank you.

01:17:58.080 --> 01:17:58.913
Thank you, Teresa.

01:17:58.913 --> 01:18:03.780
I think the Program on
Refugees does a huge job

01:18:03.780 --> 01:18:06.300
in terms of making these
stories accessible.

01:18:06.300 --> 01:18:07.380
And I agree with you.

01:18:07.380 --> 01:18:10.884
I think that the stories that Sohel

01:18:10.884 --> 01:18:13.860
and other filmmakers and
storytellers talk about

01:18:13.860 --> 01:18:16.710
is just as valuable when we say data

01:18:16.710 --> 01:18:20.820
as the kind of hard data
or quantitative data

01:18:20.820 --> 01:18:23.280
that we might do as an
academic research center.

01:18:23.280 --> 01:18:25.020
I think if you want to move people,

01:18:25.020 --> 01:18:27.000
you've got to show them
not just the numbers,

01:18:27.000 --> 01:18:29.490
but also the stories of people's lives.

01:18:29.490 --> 01:18:32.448
So I think this is fantastic.

01:18:32.448 --> 01:18:35.970
And to those on today
who are present today,

01:18:35.970 --> 01:18:37.710
I think there's a link to see the film.

01:18:37.710 --> 01:18:41.100
We'd request that you not
share it widely right now

01:18:41.100 --> 01:18:44.070
because I think it's still being shown.

01:18:44.070 --> 01:18:47.220
But, yes, we hope that you
do have a chance to catch up

01:18:47.220 --> 01:18:48.240
with this fantastic film.

01:18:48.240 --> 01:18:50.436
So thank you to everyone,

01:18:50.436 --> 01:18:53.460
to the Yale MacMillan
Center Program on Refugees

01:18:53.460 --> 01:18:54.930
for bringing this before Sohel,

01:18:54.930 --> 01:18:57.413
of course for the courage and the patience

01:18:57.413 --> 01:19:02.130
and it requires the resilience
and most of all, of course,

01:19:02.130 --> 01:19:06.300
if you ever get a chance
to speak with the children

01:19:06.300 --> 01:19:09.240
and with others in the camp
to tell them like how moved

01:19:09.240 --> 01:19:13.680
we were by the stories and
to wish them well actually.

